window.REV_FLIP=window.REV_FLIP||{};window.REV_FLIP["agentic-ai"]=[{"id":"agentic-ai-f0001","card":"q-agentic-ai-001-agentic-ai-and-agents","sec":"agentic-ai-s002","q":"What is an AI agent, and what makes a system \"agentic\"?","a":"<p>An agent is a program that perceives its environment, decides what to do from rules or learned patterns, and acts to reach a goal without a person approving each step. A system is called agentic when it pursues goals autonomously across multiple steps instead of producing one answer to one prompt.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"agentic-ai-f0002","card":"q-agentic-ai-001-agentic-ai-and-agents","sec":"agentic-ai-s002","q":"How does an agent differ from a traditional script or a plain chatbot?","a":"<p>A script follows fixed steps that a developer wrote in advance. A plain chatbot returns one reply per prompt. An agent chooses its own next step based on what it observes, can call tools or other systems, and keeps going until the goal is met or it hits a stop condition.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"agentic-ai-f0003","card":"q-agentic-ai-001-agentic-ai-and-agents","sec":"agentic-ai-s002","q":"What are the main risks of giving an agent autonomy, and how do you limit them?","a":"<p>The risks are wrong or unsafe actions at scale, unclear accountability, runaway loops and cost, and actions that cannot be undone. Limit them with clear goals and boundaries, least-privilege access to tools, rule-based guardrails, step or budget limits, logging, and a human who can approve or stop risky actions.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"agentic-ai-f0004","card":"q-agentic-ai-001-agentic-ai-and-agents","sec":"agentic-ai-s002","q":"When is a fully autonomous agent the wrong choice?","a":"<p>When the steps are known and fixed, a deterministic workflow is cheaper, faster, easier to test and more predictable. It is also the wrong choice when errors are costly or irreversible and no human can review them (for example, medical or financial decisions without oversight).</p>","hand":true},{"id":"agentic-ai-f0005","card":"q-agentic-ai-001-agentic-ai-and-agents","sec":"agentic-ai-s002","q":"What are the core components of an LLM-based agent?","a":"<p>A language model that reasons and decides, tools it can call (APIs, search, code execution), memory, and a planning or control loop that decides the next step. The runtime around the model runs the tools and feeds results back.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"agentic-ai-f0006","card":"q-agentic-ai-001-agentic-ai-and-agents","sec":"agentic-ai-s002","q":"What is the difference between short-term and long-term memory in an agent?","a":"<p>Short-term memory is the working context of the current task: the conversation, recent tool results and intermediate state, limited by the model's context window. Long-term memory persists across sessions, usually in a database or vector store, and is retrieved when relevant. Short-term memory is cheap but forgotten. Long-term memory needs storage, retrieval quality and privacy controls.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"agentic-ai-f0007","card":"q-agentic-ai-002-five-characteristics-of-agentic-ai","sec":"agentic-ai-s003","q":"What are the five characteristics that define agentic AI?","a":"<p>Independent decision-making, goal orientation, learning and adaptation, contextual understanding, and multi-domain utility. Together they mean the system acts with little supervision, works toward objectives, improves from experience, understands the situation it is in, and applies across different domains and kinds of data.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"agentic-ai-f0008","card":"q-agentic-ai-002-five-characteristics-of-agentic-ai","sec":"agentic-ai-s003","q":"What does \"goal-oriented\" mean for an agent, and why does it matter for design?","a":"<p>The agent's behavior is driven by an objective that it tries to reach, not by a single instruction. It matters because the goal decides which actions count as progress and when the agent should stop. A vague goal leads to wandering or unsafe behavior, so goals should be specific and measurable.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"agentic-ai-f0009","card":"q-agentic-ai-002-five-characteristics-of-agentic-ai","sec":"agentic-ai-s003","q":"Why is learning and adaptation listed as a core trait, and how does it show up in practice?","a":"<p>Environments change, so a fixed rule set becomes stale. An agent that learns from interactions and feedback adjusts, for example a demand-forecasting agent that notices weather affects sales and changes its orders. The trade-off is that learned behavior is harder to predict and audit than fixed rules.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"agentic-ai-f0010","card":"q-agentic-ai-002-five-characteristics-of-agentic-ai","sec":"agentic-ai-s003","q":"What does contextual understanding add to an agent's decisions?","a":"<p>It lets the agent weigh the surrounding situation (time, location, history, user intent) rather than react to raw input only. The same input can need different actions in different contexts, so context reduces false alarms and irrelevant actions.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"agentic-ai-f0011","card":"q-agentic-ai-002-five-characteristics-of-agentic-ai","sec":"agentic-ai-s003","q":"Why does independent decision-making not mean \"no human involved\"?","a":"<p>It means the agent does not need a person for each step. Humans still set goals, define policies and limits, and review or override high-risk decisions. Autonomy is usually bounded by oversight.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"agentic-ai-f0012","card":"q-agentic-ai-003-industry-use-cases","sec":"agentic-ai-s004","q":"Why do multi-agent systems often give each agent its own persona (role, responsibilities and purpose)?","a":"<p>A focused role keeps each agent's prompt and tool set small, which improves accuracy and makes behavior easier to test. It also lets agents be developed and replaced independently. The trade-off is extra coordination and more model calls.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"agentic-ai-f0013","card":"q-agentic-ai-003-industry-use-cases","sec":"agentic-ai-s004","q":"How would you design a multi-agent system for marketing analysis that gathers social media data, analyzes sentiment and writes a report?","a":"<p>Use three specialized agents in a pipeline: a collector that pulls the data through tools or APIs, an analyzer that scores sentiment, and a writer that produces the report from the analysis. Pass structured output between them, validate each stage, and add a human review of the final report before it is acted on.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"agentic-ai-f0014","card":"q-agentic-ai-003-industry-use-cases","sec":"agentic-ai-s004","q":"Which tasks in insurance suit agentic AI, and what must you add before trusting it?","a":"<p>Claims processing, customer onboarding and data extraction suit agents because they are multi-step, rule-heavy and high volume. Before trusting it, add auditability, human review for large or disputed claims, and checks for bias and regulatory compliance.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"agentic-ai-f0015","card":"q-agentic-ai-003-industry-use-cases","sec":"agentic-ai-s004","q":"Why is retail a good fit for agents that use CRM data?","a":"<p>Customer interactions across email, chat and social media produce history that an agent can use for personalized recommendations and after-sales support. The risk is privacy: access to customer data must follow consent and data-protection rules.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"agentic-ai-f0016","card":"q-agentic-ai-003-industry-use-cases","sec":"agentic-ai-s004","q":"What kinds of tasks would you give to separate healthcare agents, and why split them?","a":"<p>Typical splits are image analysis (radiology), patient-history evaluation and treatment recommendation. Splitting lets each agent use a specialized model and data source, and lets clinicians check each stage separately.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"agentic-ai-f0017","card":"q-agentic-ai-004-grey-sloan-medical-center-scenario","sec":"agentic-ai-s005","q":"In a hospital deployment, why would you separate agents into diagnostic support, patient management and administrative groups?","a":"<p>The groups have different data, risk levels and users. Diagnostic agents handle sensitive clinical data and need strict oversight. Patient management agents optimize scheduling. Administrative agents automate records and billing. Separation allows different access rights, testing standards and failure handling for each.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"agentic-ai-f0018","card":"q-agentic-ai-004-grey-sloan-medical-center-scenario","sec":"agentic-ai-s005","q":"How can a scheduling agent reduce cancellations and waiting times?","a":"<p>It matches appointment slots to provider availability and patient needs using real-time data, fills gaps freed by cancellations, and can send reminders or reschedule automatically. The agent optimizes a utility such as provider time used and wait time, within constraints set by staff.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"agentic-ai-f0019","card":"q-agentic-ai-005-diagnostic-support-agent-flow","sec":"agentic-ai-s006","q":"In a clinical decision-support agent, why should the final decision stay with the physician?","a":"<p>The agent can be wrong, and its recommendation is based on patterns in data, not on accountability for the patient. Keeping a human in the loop supports safety, legal responsibility and trust. The agent augments the clinician by surfacing findings and options.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"agentic-ai-f0020","card":"q-agentic-ai-005-diagnostic-support-agent-flow","sec":"agentic-ai-s006","q":"Describe the data flow of a diagnostic support agent from input to output.","a":"<p>Inputs such as radiology images, lab results and patient history are collected. ML models analyze them. The agent produces a recommendation (for example, a finding and possible treatments) and sends it to the physician in near real time. The physician reviews it and makes the decision.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"agentic-ai-f0021","card":"q-agentic-ai-005-diagnostic-support-agent-flow","sec":"agentic-ai-s006","q":"Which risks must you address before deploying an agent that analyzes patient data?","a":"<p>Privacy and consent (health data is regulated), bias in training data, accuracy and false negatives, explainability for clinicians, and an audit trail of what the agent recommended. Security of data in transit and at rest also matters.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"agentic-ai-f0022","card":"q-agentic-ai-006-environmental-interaction","sec":"agentic-ai-s007","q":"What is the perceive-interpret-decide-act cycle in an agent?","a":"<p>Sensors or inputs collect data, the agent interprets it into useful information, decides what to do, acts, and then senses the result of that action. Feedback from the result feeds the next cycle, so the loop repeats.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"agentic-ai-f0023","card":"q-agentic-ai-006-environmental-interaction","sec":"agentic-ai-s007","q":"What types of data can an agent perceive, and why does the data type matter?","a":"<p>Images, audio, video and other sensor readings. The type decides which models and preprocessing you need (for example, vision models for images, speech or audio models for voicemail), and the data volume and latency requirements.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"agentic-ai-f0024","card":"q-agentic-ai-006-environmental-interaction","sec":"agentic-ai-s007","q":"Why does noisy or incomplete sensor data matter for an agent, and how do you handle it?","a":"<p>Decisions are only as good as the perception behind them, so noise causes wrong actions. Common measures are filtering and validation, combining multiple sensors, confidence thresholds before acting, and asking for human review when confidence is low.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"agentic-ai-f0025","card":"q-agentic-ai-006-environmental-interaction","sec":"agentic-ai-s007","q":"How do environment types such as fully versus partially observable change agent design?","a":"<p>In a fully observable environment the current input holds everything needed, so a simple reactive agent can work. In a partially observable one the agent needs memory or an internal model of hidden state. Stochastic environments need probabilistic reasoning or expected utility, dynamic ones (that change while the agent thinks) need fast decisions and replanning, and deterministic static ones allow plain search and planning.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"agentic-ai-f0026","card":"q-agentic-ai-006-environmental-interaction","sec":"agentic-ai-s007","q":"What is the difference between a deterministic and a stochastic environment for an agent?","a":"<p>In a deterministic environment the same action in the same state always gives the same result. In a stochastic one the result is random with some probability. Stochastic environments mean a plan can fail even when executed correctly, so the agent needs to check outcomes and handle failure.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"agentic-ai-f0027","card":"q-agentic-ai-007-feedback-loop","sec":"agentic-ai-s008","q":"What is a feedback loop in an agentic system, and what problem does it solve?","a":"<p>It is the cycle of acting, observing the outcome, receiving a correction and updating behavior. It solves the problem of repeating the same mistake, so the agent improves over time instead of staying at its initial accuracy.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"agentic-ai-f0028","card":"q-agentic-ai-007-feedback-loop","sec":"agentic-ai-s008","q":"How would a vision-based security agent use user feedback to reduce false alerts?","a":"<p>When the user marks an alert as a false alarm, that label becomes training or tuning data. The agent updates its model or thresholds so similar patterns are less likely to be flagged. This needs a reliable way for users to give feedback and a way to retrain or update safely.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"agentic-ai-f0029","card":"q-agentic-ai-007-feedback-loop","sec":"agentic-ai-s008","q":"What can go wrong with an agent that learns from feedback?","a":"<p>Biased or malicious feedback can teach wrong behavior, and feedback can reinforce its own mistakes (a feedback loop that amplifies errors). Mitigate it by validating feedback, monitoring metrics, keeping versioned models you can roll back, and testing before deploying updates.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"agentic-ai-f0030","card":"q-agentic-ai-007-feedback-loop","sec":"agentic-ai-s008","q":"What are the four stages of a feedback cycle?","a":"<p>Action taken, outcome sensed, feedback received, and learning. The agent makes an assessment, observes the result, gets a correction from a user or the system, and updates so it does not repeat the error.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"agentic-ai-f0031","card":"q-agentic-ai-008-agent-to-agent-communication","sec":"agentic-ai-s009","q":"What are three ways agents can communicate, and when would you choose each?","a":"<p>Messaging (agents send commands or data to each other) fits loosely coupled, event-driven work. Shared databases (persistent data that agents read and write) fit state that must survive and be seen by many agents. Direct interaction (a durable connection) fits low-latency, tightly coupled exchanges.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"agentic-ai-f0032","card":"q-agentic-ai-008-agent-to-agent-communication","sec":"agentic-ai-s009","q":"What are the trade-offs of using a shared database for agent communication compared with direct messaging?","a":"<p>A shared database gives persistence and a single source of truth, and agents need not be online at the same time. The costs are possible contention and conflicting writes, higher latency, and tight coupling to the data schema. Messaging is faster and decoupled but needs delivery guarantees and does not persist state by itself.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"agentic-ai-f0033","card":"q-agentic-ai-009-agent-coordination","sec":"agentic-ai-s010","q":"What is agent coordination, and why is it needed in a multi-agent system?","a":"<p>Coordination is the set of strategies and processes that manage how many agents interact. It is needed to avoid conflicts and redundant work, order dependent tasks, and make the agents' actions add up to the shared goal.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"agentic-ai-f0034","card":"q-agentic-ai-009-agent-coordination","sec":"agentic-ai-s010","q":"How would you split a video-surveillance system into cooperating agents?","a":"<p>One agent monitors the video feed, a second detects objects that need identification, and a third classifies what each object is and decides on an action. Each agent has one job, and they pass results along. This keeps each component simple, and you can improve or replace one without changing the others.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"agentic-ai-f0035","card":"q-agentic-ai-009-agent-coordination","sec":"agentic-ai-s010","q":"What problems appear if coordination is missing in a multi-agent system?","a":"<p>Agents may do the same task twice, take contradictory actions, deadlock while waiting for each other, or act on outdated shared state. Coordination mechanisms such as roles, a leader, locks or consensus prevent this.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"agentic-ai-f0036","card":"q-agentic-ai-010-decision-making-flow","sec":"agentic-ai-s011","q":"What are the stages of an agent's planning and decision flow?","a":"<p>Goal definition, action sequencing, environmental modeling and continual planning. The agent defines what it wants, orders the actions to reach it, models the environment to predict results, and keeps updating the plan as things change.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"agentic-ai-f0037","card":"q-agentic-ai-010-decision-making-flow","sec":"agentic-ai-s011","q":"Why must goals be defined before deployment and not discovered at run time?","a":"<p>The goal decides which actions are allowed, how success is measured and when to stop. If it is undefined, the agent cannot be tested, may pursue unwanted objectives and cannot be held within safety limits.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"agentic-ai-f0038","card":"q-agentic-ai-010-decision-making-flow","sec":"agentic-ai-s011","q":"Why does an agent that makes a plan once and never revises it fail in a real environment?","a":"<p>Environments change and observations are imperfect. A static plan keeps executing steps that no longer make sense. Agents need a plan-act-observe-replan loop so they adapt.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"agentic-ai-f0039","card":"q-agentic-ai-011-goal-definition","sec":"agentic-ai-s012","q":"What makes a well-defined goal for an agent?","a":"<p>It is specific, measurable, bounded by constraints and has a clear end condition. For example, \"navigate the car to a given address within traffic rules\" is better than \"drive well\". Clear goals allow testing and prevent unintended behavior.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"agentic-ai-f0040","card":"q-agentic-ai-011-goal-definition","sec":"agentic-ai-s012","q":"How does the complexity of a goal change the design of an agent?","a":"<p>A simple goal (detect a moving target) can use one model and a short action chain. A complex goal (handle an accident scene) needs decomposition into sub-goals, planning, several tools or agents, and human escalation. Complex goals raise coordination and safety needs.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"agentic-ai-f0041","card":"q-agentic-ai-011-goal-definition","sec":"agentic-ai-s012","q":"What is the risk of a poorly specified or misaligned goal?","a":"<p>The agent can optimize the literal objective in unintended ways, for example maximizing a metric while violating the intent. This is why goals need constraints, policy rules and human review.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"agentic-ai-f0042","card":"q-agentic-ai-012-action-sequencing","sec":"agentic-ai-s013","q":"What is action sequencing, and why does order matter?","a":"<p>It is defining the ordered actions that take the agent from the current state to the goal state. Order matters because later steps depend on earlier results, for example you must detect a person before you can analyze their intent, and assess risk before you decide on an alert.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"agentic-ai-f0043","card":"q-agentic-ai-012-action-sequencing","sec":"agentic-ai-s013","q":"Design the action sequence for a security agent that must decide whether to alert authorities.","a":"<p>Observe the area, detect people, analyze them, infer intent, assess risk, weigh trade-offs (for example, a false alarm against a missed threat), and then take the predefined action such as alerting the operator or authorities. Each step feeds the next, and the final action should respect policy limits.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"agentic-ai-f0044","card":"q-agentic-ai-012-action-sequencing","sec":"agentic-ai-s013","q":"Why should risk and trade-offs be evaluated before an agent takes an irreversible action?","a":"<p>A wrong irreversible action cannot be fixed afterwards, so the agent should estimate the cost of error first and escalate to a human when confidence is low or impact is high.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"agentic-ai-f0045","card":"q-agentic-ai-013-environmental-modeling","sec":"agentic-ai-s014","q":"What is environmental modeling, and what does it let an agent do?","a":"<p>It is building a digital representation of the agent's environment. The agent can simulate possible actions and outcomes inside the model and pick the best one before acting in the real world.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"agentic-ai-f0046","card":"q-agentic-ai-013-environmental-modeling","sec":"agentic-ai-s014","q":"What is the benefit and the limit of planning with an internal model of the environment?","a":"<p>The benefit is safe, cheap trial and error and the ability to look ahead. The limit is that a model is only an approximation, so errors or outdated assumptions in it produce bad plans. The model must be updated from observations.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"agentic-ai-f0047","card":"q-agentic-ai-013-environmental-modeling","sec":"agentic-ai-s014","q":"How is a model-based agent different from a purely reactive one?","a":"<p>A reactive agent maps the current input directly to an action. A model-based agent keeps an internal model and state, which lets it handle things it cannot see right now and plan several steps ahead, at the price of more computation.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"agentic-ai-f0048","card":"q-agentic-ai-014-continual-planning","sec":"agentic-ai-s015","q":"What is continual planning, and why is it needed?","a":"<p>It is updating and revising the plan as new information arrives and the environment changes. It is needed because the original plan assumed a particular situation, and the world rarely stays that way.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"agentic-ai-f0049","card":"q-agentic-ai-014-continual-planning","sec":"agentic-ai-s015","q":"A monitoring agent notices that the environment it modeled has changed. How should continual planning handle it?","a":"<p>The agent re-evaluates the scene, updates its environment model, and re-plans only the actions the change affects. It does not need to restart everything.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"agentic-ai-f0050","card":"q-agentic-ai-014-continual-planning","sec":"agentic-ai-s015","q":"What is the trade-off between replanning on every change and replanning only on major changes?","a":"<p>Replanning constantly keeps the plan accurate but costs compute and can cause unstable behavior. Replanning only on major changes is cheap but may act on stale assumptions. A common compromise is a threshold or trigger-based replanning.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"agentic-ai-f0051","card":"q-agentic-ai-015-ml-versus-agentic-ai-frameworks","sec":"agentic-ai-s016","q":"What is the relationship between machine learning and agentic AI?","a":"<p>Machine learning is the methodology of learning patterns from data to make predictions or decisions. Agentic AI uses ML as a component and adds goals, decision loops, tools and autonomy. ML is a model. An agent is a system built around models that acts.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"agentic-ai-f0052","card":"q-agentic-ai-015-ml-versus-agentic-ai-frameworks","sec":"agentic-ai-s016","q":"Why is a trained ML model alone not an agent?","a":"<p>A model maps inputs to outputs. It does not pursue goals, choose actions in an environment, or observe the results. An agent wraps models in a loop of perceiving, deciding and acting.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"agentic-ai-f0053","card":"q-agentic-ai-015-ml-versus-agentic-ai-frameworks","sec":"agentic-ai-s016","q":"Why use an agent framework instead of calling an ML model directly?","a":"<p>A framework provides the surrounding machinery: tool calling, memory, planning loops, state, error handling and multi-agent coordination. Building these yourself costs time, though a framework also adds abstraction and dependencies.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"agentic-ai-f0054","card":"q-agentic-ai-016-three-main-ml-models","sec":"agentic-ai-s017","q":"What is the difference between supervised, unsupervised and reinforcement learning?","a":"<p>Supervised learning learns from labeled examples to predict labels. Unsupervised learning finds structure in unlabeled data. Reinforcement learning learns by acting in an environment and getting rewards or penalties.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"agentic-ai-f0055","card":"q-agentic-ai-016-three-main-ml-models","sec":"agentic-ai-s017","q":"Which type of ML would you use to group customers into segments without labels, and why?","a":"<p>Unsupervised learning, for example clustering, because there are no labeled targets and you want to discover natural groups.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"agentic-ai-f0056","card":"q-agentic-ai-016-three-main-ml-models","sec":"agentic-ai-s017","q":"Which type of ML fits an agent that must learn a policy by trial and error, and why is it harder than supervised learning?","a":"<p>Reinforcement learning. It is harder because rewards are often delayed, the agent must explore as well as exploit, training needs many interactions or a simulator, and there is no correct label for each step.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"agentic-ai-f0057","card":"q-agentic-ai-016-three-main-ml-models","sec":"agentic-ai-s017","q":"You need to classify images as cat or dog and have thousands of labeled images. Which learning type, and what is the main cost?","a":"<p>Supervised learning. The main cost is getting and maintaining accurate labels, and the model only generalizes to data similar to the training set.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"agentic-ai-f0058","card":"q-agentic-ai-017-ml-inside-agents","sec":"agentic-ai-s018","q":"What are the two ways an agent can use ML, and when would you pick each?","a":"<p>Embedded, where the model is part of the agent, and external, where the agent calls a model as a service. Embedded gives low latency and works offline, which suits edge devices. External is easier to update and scale, and gives access to large models, but adds network latency, cost and a dependency.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"agentic-ai-f0059","card":"q-agentic-ai-017-ml-inside-agents","sec":"agentic-ai-s018","q":"What role does ML play in letting an agent act autonomously?","a":"<p>It lets the agent evaluate actions, predict outcomes, optimize toward its goals and handle situations that fixed rules do not cover.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"agentic-ai-f0060","card":"q-agentic-ai-017-ml-inside-agents","sec":"agentic-ai-s018","q":"What is the trade-off between rule-based logic and ML inside an agent?","a":"<p>Rules are predictable, auditable and easy to guarantee, but brittle outside the cases they cover. ML adapts to patterns and handles ambiguity, but is probabilistic and harder to explain. Many systems use ML for perception and rules for safety limits.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"agentic-ai-f0061","card":"q-agentic-ai-018-continuous-improvement-cycle","sec":"agentic-ai-s019","q":"What is the continuous improvement cycle for an agent with embedded ML?","a":"<p>Process new data, refine performance metrics, improve prediction accuracy, face new challenges with more robustness, and repeat. The agent keeps getting better as it is exposed to more data.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"agentic-ai-f0062","card":"q-agentic-ai-018-continuous-improvement-cycle","sec":"agentic-ai-s019","q":"What do you need in place to let a deployed agent improve safely over time?","a":"<p>Monitoring of quality metrics, validated and versioned data, evaluation before each model update, a way to roll back, and protection against drift and poisoned data. Without these, continuous learning can degrade the system.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"agentic-ai-f0063","card":"q-agentic-ai-019-ml-business-applications","sec":"agentic-ai-s020","q":"Why is ML a good fit for fraud detection and anomaly detection?","a":"<p>Fraud and anomalies are patterns in large volumes of data that are hard to describe with fixed rules. ML learns normal behavior and flags deviations. The challenges are class imbalance (fraud is rare), false positives and adversaries who change tactics.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"agentic-ai-f0064","card":"q-agentic-ai-020-what-policy-frameworks-are","sec":"agentic-ai-s021","q":"What is a policy framework for an agent, and why is it needed?","a":"<p>It is a set of guidelines that controls and directs the agent's actions, aligned with the values, laws and norms set by the organization. It is needed because an autonomous agent without constraints could, for example, share personal data without consent or perform illegal or unethical actions.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"agentic-ai-f0065","card":"q-agentic-ai-020-what-policy-frameworks-are","sec":"agentic-ai-s021","q":"What is the difference between a decision-making framework and a rule-based framework?","a":"<p>A decision-making framework aligns the agent's actions with ethical, legal and societal standards through policies, oversight and risk assessment. A rule-based framework defines explicit rules the agent must follow for predictable, compliant behavior. The first sets principles and governance. The second enforces concrete behavior.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"agentic-ai-f0066","card":"q-agentic-ai-020-what-policy-frameworks-are","sec":"agentic-ai-s021","q":"Who defines the policies an agent follows?","a":"<p>Architects, designers, developers, the organization's legal and compliance teams and sometimes end users. Policies reflect business, legal and ethical requirements, and they need an owner who keeps them current.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"agentic-ai-f0067","card":"q-agentic-ai-021-decision-making-framework-components","sec":"agentic-ai-s022","q":"What are the components of a decision-making framework for agents?","a":"<p>Ethical policies, oversight mechanisms, and risk assessment and mitigation. Ethical policies align actions with law and social norms. Oversight keeps humans able to monitor and stop the agent. Risk assessment identifies and reduces adverse outcomes.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"agentic-ai-f0068","card":"q-agentic-ai-021-decision-making-framework-components","sec":"agentic-ai-s022","q":"What is the difference between human-in-the-loop and human-on-the-loop?","a":"<p>In human-in-the-loop, a person takes part in each decision or must approve it before the agent acts. In human-on-the-loop, the agent acts on its own while a person supervises and can intervene when needed. Choose in-the-loop for high-risk or irreversible decisions, on-the-loop where speed and scale matter and mistakes are recoverable.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"agentic-ai-f0069","card":"q-agentic-ai-021-decision-making-framework-components","sec":"agentic-ai-s022","q":"Why would you build a kill switch into an autonomous system?","a":"<p>It lets operators stop the agent immediately if it misbehaves, acts outside its limits or causes harm. It is a basic oversight mechanism and should work independently of the agent's own logic.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"agentic-ai-f0070","card":"q-agentic-ai-022-rule-based-framework","sec":"agentic-ai-s023","q":"What are the strengths and weaknesses of a rule-based framework for agent behavior?","a":"<p>Strengths: predictable, easy to audit, and guaranteed compliance with explicit constraints. Weaknesses: it cannot handle situations the rules did not foresee, and large rule sets become hard to maintain. It works well as a safety layer around a more flexible component.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"agentic-ai-f0071","card":"q-agentic-ai-022-rule-based-framework","sec":"agentic-ai-s023","q":"How can an agent adapt to changing conditions and still obey fixed rules?","a":"<p>Separate hard constraints from adjustable behavior. The agent may change parameters such as a material mix with the weather, but only inside the limits the rules define. Hard rules (for example, never harm a human) are checked before any action runs and always override the adaptive part.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"agentic-ai-f0072","card":"q-agentic-ai-022-rule-based-framework","sec":"agentic-ai-s023","q":"What is a hard constraint, and why enforce it outside the learned model?","a":"<p>A hard constraint is a rule that must never be broken regardless of the agent's reasoning. Enforcing it in separate code, not only in a prompt or a learned policy, means a model error or manipulation cannot bypass it.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"agentic-ai-f0073","card":"q-agentic-ai-023-ongoing-monitoring","sec":"agentic-ai-s024","q":"Why are policy frameworks not set-and-forget?","a":"<p>Laws, business needs, and the agent's behavior and environment all change. Policies must be revisited, and the agent's compliance checked, or the system drifts out of alignment.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"agentic-ai-f0074","card":"q-agentic-ai-023-ongoing-monitoring","sec":"agentic-ai-s024","q":"What is the lifecycle for managing agent policies?","a":"<p>Set the policies, deploy the agent, monitor its actions, check compliance, then review and update the policies, and repeat. Findings from monitoring feed the next version of the policies.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"agentic-ai-f0075","card":"q-agentic-ai-023-ongoing-monitoring","sec":"agentic-ai-s024","q":"What would you monitor to verify that an autonomous agent is following its policies?","a":"<p>Actions taken and the reasoning or inputs behind them, policy violations and blocked actions, error and escalation rates, outcome quality and any change in behavior over time. Keep logs for audit.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"agentic-ai-f0076","card":"q-agentic-ai-024-utility-based-frameworks","sec":"agentic-ai-s025","q":"What is a utility-based agent, and how does it differ from a goal-based agent?","a":"<p>A goal-based agent only checks whether a goal is reached or not. A utility-based agent assigns a numeric value (utility) to outcomes and picks the action with the best expected value, so it can compare how good different ways of reaching a goal are and balance competing objectives.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"agentic-ai-f0077","card":"q-agentic-ai-024-utility-based-frameworks","sec":"agentic-ai-s025","q":"What are the main components of a utility-based framework?","a":"<p>Utility functions, preference modeling, risk and uncertainty handling, and trade-off analysis. Together they let the agent score outcomes, reflect user preferences, reason about probabilities and balance conflicting objectives.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"agentic-ai-f0078","card":"q-agentic-ai-024-utility-based-frameworks","sec":"agentic-ai-s025","q":"When would you prefer a utility-based approach to simple rules?","a":"<p>When there are several competing objectives, uncertain outcomes, or many acceptable choices that differ in quality. Rules fit when the correct action is clear. Utility fits when you need to rank and trade off options. The cost is designing and validating the utility function.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"agentic-ai-f0079","card":"q-agentic-ai-025-utility-functions-and-preference-modelin","sec":"agentic-ai-s026","q":"What does a utility function do in an agent?","a":"<p>It maps each possible outcome to a number representing how desirable it is. The agent compares the numbers and picks the action with the highest expected utility.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"agentic-ai-f0080","card":"q-agentic-ai-025-utility-functions-and-preference-modelin","sec":"agentic-ai-s026","q":"A camera agent scores a detected person with an object from 1 (unlikely a weapon) to 10 (very likely a weapon). How does this score drive behavior?","a":"<p>The score is compared with thresholds. A low score means ignore, a middle score means keep observing to gather more information, and a high score triggers an alert or escalation. The thresholds set the balance between false alarms and missed threats.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"agentic-ai-f0081","card":"q-agentic-ai-025-utility-functions-and-preference-modelin","sec":"agentic-ai-s026","q":"What is preference modeling, and why feed it into the utility function?","a":"<p>Preference modeling captures what the user or business values and turns it into weights or parameters in the utility function. It makes the agent's choices match what people actually want and not a generic optimum.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"agentic-ai-f0082","card":"q-agentic-ai-025-utility-functions-and-preference-modelin","sec":"agentic-ai-s026","q":"What goes wrong with a badly designed utility function?","a":"<p>The agent optimizes exactly what the function says, so missing factors or wrong weights lead to harmful or silly choices (reward hacking or specification gaming). Test it on edge cases and include constraints.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"agentic-ai-f0083","card":"q-agentic-ai-026-risk-uncertainty-and-trade-offs","sec":"agentic-ai-s027","q":"Why do utility-based agents need probabilistic models?","a":"<p>Outcomes are uncertain, so the agent must weigh each outcome by its probability. Expected utility is the sum of utility times probability, which lets the agent compare risky options rationally.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"agentic-ai-f0084","card":"q-agentic-ai-026-risk-uncertainty-and-trade-offs","sec":"agentic-ai-s027","q":"What is trade-off analysis in an agent, and can you give an example?","a":"<p>It is balancing competing objectives and constraints, such as speed against accuracy, or false alarms against missed detections. A simple example is carrying an umbrella: it costs a little effort but protects against rain. The agent chooses the option with the best overall value for the stakeholders' preferences.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"agentic-ai-f0085","card":"q-agentic-ai-026-risk-uncertainty-and-trade-offs","sec":"agentic-ai-s027","q":"Why does stakeholder risk tolerance matter when you tune an agent?","a":"<p>Different stakeholders accept different levels of risk. A medical agent should lean toward not missing disease even at the cost of more false alarms, while a marketing agent can tolerate errors. Thresholds and utility weights should reflect that tolerance.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"agentic-ai-f0086","card":"q-agentic-ai-026-risk-uncertainty-and-trade-offs","sec":"agentic-ai-s027","q":"How can bias enter an agent's risk analysis, and what should you do?","a":"<p>Skewed training data or poorly chosen features can make the model's probability estimates wrong for some groups. Account for bias explicitly, test across groups and review the results with stakeholders.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"agentic-ai-f0087","card":"q-agentic-ai-027-reinforcement-learning","sec":"agentic-ai-s028","q":"How does reinforcement learning work?","a":"<p>An agent observes a state, takes an action, and the environment returns a reward or penalty and a new state. Over many interactions the agent learns a policy that maximizes cumulative reward.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"agentic-ai-f0088","card":"q-agentic-ai-027-reinforcement-learning","sec":"agentic-ai-s028","q":"What is the exploration versus exploitation trade-off in reinforcement learning?","a":"<p>Exploitation uses the best action known so far to collect reward. Exploration tries other actions that might turn out better. Too much exploitation can lock in a poor policy, and too much exploration wastes reward. Strategies such as epsilon-greedy balance them.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"agentic-ai-f0089","card":"q-agentic-ai-027-reinforcement-learning","sec":"agentic-ai-s028","q":"Why is reward design critical in reinforcement learning?","a":"<p>The agent maximizes the reward it is given, not what you meant. A poorly designed reward can be exploited in unintended ways. Rewards should reflect the real objective and include penalties for unsafe behavior.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"agentic-ai-f0090","card":"q-agentic-ai-027-reinforcement-learning","sec":"agentic-ai-s028","q":"Why is RL usually trained in a simulator for tasks like autonomous driving?","a":"<p>Learning by trial and error in the real world is unsafe, slow and expensive. A simulator lets the agent make millions of mistakes safely. The gap between simulation and reality still has to be handled.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"agentic-ai-f0091","card":"q-agentic-ai-027-reinforcement-learning","sec":"agentic-ai-s028","q":"How does reinforcement learning differ from supervised learning in terms of feedback?","a":"<p>Supervised learning gets the correct answer for each example. RL gets only a scalar reward, often delayed, that says how good the actions were overall, so the agent must work out which earlier actions earned it.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"agentic-ai-f0092","card":"q-agentic-ai-028-q-learning-and-the-q-table","sec":"agentic-ai-s029","q":"What is Q-learning, and what is the Q-table?","a":"<p>Q-learning is a reinforcement learning algorithm that learns the value of taking each action in each state. The Q-table stores those values, one row per state and one column per action. The agent picks the action with the highest value and updates the entry from experience.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"agentic-ai-f0093","card":"q-agentic-ai-028-q-learning-and-the-q-table","sec":"agentic-ai-s029","q":"How is a Q-table updated after an action? Show the update rule.","a":"<p>The new value moves the old value toward a target: old + alpha * (reward + gamma * best value of the next state - old). Alpha is the learning rate and gamma is the discount factor. The max is taken over all actions available in the next state, not just one fixed action. In the snippet the best next value is 0.8, so the entry becomes 0.0 + 0.1 * (1.0 + 0.9 * 0.8 - 0.0) = 0.172.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>alpha, gamma = 0.1, 0.9\nactions = [&quot;left&quot;, &quot;right&quot;]\nq = {(&quot;A&quot;, &quot;left&quot;): 0.0, (&quot;B&quot;, &quot;left&quot;): 0.5, (&quot;B&quot;, &quot;right&quot;): 0.8}\nstate, action, reward, next_state = &quot;A&quot;, &quot;left&quot;, 1.0, &quot;B&quot;\nbest_next = max(q.get((next_state, a), 0.0) for a in actions)\nq[(state, action)] = q[(state, action)] + alpha * (\n    reward + gamma * best_next - q[(state, action)]\n)</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"agentic-ai-f0094","card":"q-agentic-ai-028-q-learning-and-the-q-table","sec":"agentic-ai-s029","q":"What are the limitations of a Q-table?","a":"<p>It only works with small, discrete state and action spaces, because the table grows with the number of states and actions. For large or continuous spaces you approximate Q with a neural network (deep Q-networks).</p>","hand":true},{"id":"agentic-ai-f0095","card":"q-agentic-ai-028-q-learning-and-the-q-table","sec":"agentic-ai-s029","q":"Q-learning is called off-policy. What does that mean?","a":"<p>It learns the value of the best action in the next state (a max) regardless of which action the agent actually takes while exploring. That lets it learn the optimal policy while still exploring.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"agentic-ai-f0096","card":"q-agentic-ai-028-q-learning-and-the-q-table","sec":"agentic-ai-s029","q":"How does epsilon-greedy action selection work in Q-learning?","a":"<p>With probability epsilon the agent picks a random action (explore), and otherwise it picks the action with the highest Q-value (exploit). Epsilon usually starts high and decays as the table becomes reliable. Without exploration the agent can lock in a poor action it tried early.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"agentic-ai-f0097","card":"q-agentic-ai-028-q-learning-and-the-q-table","sec":"agentic-ai-s029","q":"What is the idea behind the Q-learning update (the Bellman equation)?","a":"<p>The value of an action should equal its immediate reward plus the discounted value of the best action in the next state. The update nudges the current estimate toward that target by the learning rate. Repeating it over many steps propagates reward information backward through the states.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"agentic-ai-f0098","card":"q-agentic-ai-028-q-learning-and-the-q-table","sec":"agentic-ai-s029","q":"How does Q-learning differ from SARSA?","a":"<p>Q-learning is off-policy: its target uses the max over next actions, whatever the agent actually does next. SARSA is on-policy: its target uses the action the agent actually takes next under its exploring policy. Because SARSA includes the effect of exploration, it tends to learn safer paths near risky states, while Q-learning learns the optimal greedy policy.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"agentic-ai-f0099","card":"q-agentic-ai-029-six-steps-to-develop-a-q-learning-algori","sec":"agentic-ai-s030","q":"What are the steps to set up a Q-learning algorithm?","a":"<p>Define the environment, identify the states, define the actions, establish the reward signal, develop the policy, and define the value function. Then let the agent interact, update the Q-table and improve.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"agentic-ai-f0100","card":"q-agentic-ai-029-six-steps-to-develop-a-q-learning-algori","sec":"agentic-ai-s030","q":"Why must you define states and actions before the reward signal in Q-learning?","a":"<p>The reward is a function of a state and an action, so you need to know what states and actions exist before you can say what each one earns. The Q-table's rows and columns also come from them.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"agentic-ai-f0101","card":"q-agentic-ai-029-six-steps-to-develop-a-q-learning-algori","sec":"agentic-ai-s030","q":"What are the roles of the policy and the value function in Q-learning?","a":"<p>The policy maps states to actions and decides what the agent does. The value function estimates the expected cumulative reward from a state under that policy, and tells the agent how good a situation is in the long run.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"agentic-ai-f0102","card":"q-agentic-ai-029-six-steps-to-develop-a-q-learning-algori","sec":"agentic-ai-s030","q":"What does the discount factor control in Q-learning?","a":"<p>It sets how much future rewards count compared with immediate ones. A value near 0 makes the agent short-sighted. A value near 1 makes it value long-term reward more.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"agentic-ai-f0103","card":"q-agentic-ai-030-key-rl-terminology","sec":"agentic-ai-s031","q":"Define state, action, reward, policy and value function in reinforcement learning.","a":"<p>State is the current situation of the environment. Action is what the agent can do. Reward is numerical feedback after an action. Policy is the strategy mapping states to actions. Value function is the expected cumulative reward from a state when following a policy.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"agentic-ai-f0104","card":"q-agentic-ai-030-key-rl-terminology","sec":"agentic-ai-s031","q":"What is the difference between reward and value in reinforcement learning?","a":"<p>Reward is immediate feedback for one step. Value is the expected total reward over the future from a state. An action with a small reward now can have high value if it leads to larger rewards later.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"agentic-ai-f0105","card":"q-agentic-ai-030-key-rl-terminology","sec":"agentic-ai-s031","q":"What is the difference between a policy and a Q-table?","a":"<p>A policy is the rule for choosing actions. A Q-table stores estimated action values, and the policy can be derived from it by choosing the highest-value action in each state. Not every RL method uses a Q-table.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"agentic-ai-f0106","card":"q-agentic-ai-031-major-versus-minor-architectures","sec":"agentic-ai-s032","q":"What is the difference between major and minor agent architectures?","a":"<p>A major architecture is the overall strategic design that controls the agent, broad and foundational. A minor architecture is a narrower, specialized pattern that handles specific tasks on behalf of the major one, such as updating firmware or adjusting a sensor.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"agentic-ai-f0107","card":"q-agentic-ai-031-major-versus-minor-architectures","sec":"agentic-ai-s032","q":"Why does the choice of architecture matter?","a":"<p>It determines how the agent processes information, learns and makes decisions, and so its capabilities, cost, latency and how hard it is to test and debug.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"agentic-ai-f0108","card":"q-agentic-ai-031-major-versus-minor-architectures","sec":"agentic-ai-s032","q":"How would you choose an architecture for a new agent?","a":"<p>Start with the simplest one that fits the task. Use a single agent for well-defined problems, add tools when it needs outside data or actions, add reflection if output quality matters, planning for long tasks, and multiple agents when specialization or parallel work is needed. Add human collaboration for high-risk decisions.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"agentic-ai-f0109","card":"q-agentic-ai-032-seven-major-architectures","sec":"agentic-ai-s033","q":"How do the architectures compare in complexity and when would you add each?","a":"<p>Single-agent is simplest and fits well-defined tasks. Tool-integrated adds external capabilities. Reflection adds a self-critique loop for quality. Planning-centric handles long multi-step tasks. Multi-agent adds specialization and parallel work at the cost of coordination. Human-AI collaboration adds oversight where risk is high.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"agentic-ai-f0110","card":"q-agentic-ai-032-seven-major-architectures","sec":"agentic-ai-s033","q":"Can architectures be combined in one system? Give an example.","a":"<p>Yes. For example, a multi-agent system where each agent is tool-integrated and uses reflection, with a human approving the final decision. Real systems usually mix patterns.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"agentic-ai-f0111","card":"q-agentic-ai-033-single-agent-and-multi-agent","sec":"agentic-ai-s034","q":"When is a single-agent architecture enough?","a":"<p>When the problem is well defined, the tool set is small, and no collaboration or feedback from other agents is needed. It is simpler to build, test and debug, and cheaper to run.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"agentic-ai-f0112","card":"q-agentic-ai-033-single-agent-and-multi-agent","sec":"agentic-ai-s034","q":"What are the advantages and costs of a multi-agent architecture over a single agent?","a":"<p>Advantages: specialized agents with focused instructions, parallel work, independent development and resilience. Costs: more model calls, latency and cost, coordination logic, harder debugging and the risk of conflicting actions.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"agentic-ai-f0113","card":"q-agentic-ai-033-single-agent-and-multi-agent","sec":"agentic-ai-s034","q":"What are signs that a single agent should be split into several agents?","a":"<p>One prompt or tool set has grown too large and quality drops, tasks need different expertise or permissions, parts can run in parallel, or you want to test and replace components independently.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"agentic-ai-f0114","card":"q-agentic-ai-033-single-agent-and-multi-agent","sec":"agentic-ai-s034","q":"What are common orchestration topologies for multi-agent systems, and how do you choose?","a":"<p>In a supervisor (hierarchical) topology one agent routes tasks to workers and merges results. In a handoff topology the active agent passes control to another agent. In a peer-to-peer topology agents talk to each other directly. A supervisor is easiest to control and debug but is a bottleneck. Handoffs suit sequential workflows. Peer-to-peer is flexible but hardest to predict and trace. Start with the simplest topology that meets the need.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"agentic-ai-f0115","card":"q-agentic-ai-034-reflection-based-and-tool-integrated","sec":"agentic-ai-s035","q":"What is a reflection-based agent, and what problem does it solve?","a":"<p>It produces an output, critiques it, and refines it in a loop until it is validated. It reduces errors such as false positives, since the agent checks its own conclusion before acting. The cost is extra model calls and latency.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"agentic-ai-f0116","card":"q-agentic-ai-034-reflection-based-and-tool-integrated","sec":"agentic-ai-s035","q":"What is the weakness of relying only on self-critique in a reflection loop?","a":"<p>The same model may share the same blind spots, so it can approve its own mistakes. Use an independent checker, ground the critique in external evidence or tests, and cap the number of iterations.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"agentic-ai-f0117","card":"q-agentic-ai-034-reflection-based-and-tool-integrated","sec":"agentic-ai-s035","q":"Why give an agent external tools, and what are the risks?","a":"<p>Tools let the agent go beyond its fixed knowledge and abilities: search the web, process images, call APIs or run code. The risks are unsafe or wrong calls, injection of malicious content from tool results, leaking data, and cost. Mitigate with least privilege, input validation, allowlists and logging.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"agentic-ai-f0118","card":"q-agentic-ai-034-reflection-based-and-tool-integrated","sec":"agentic-ai-s035","q":"Why does tool-integrated design need clear tool descriptions?","a":"<p>The agent selects tools from their descriptions and parameters. Vague descriptions lead to wrong tool choices or wrong arguments. Precise names, purposes and parameter schemas improve reliability.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"agentic-ai-f0119","card":"q-agentic-ai-034-reflection-based-and-tool-integrated","sec":"agentic-ai-s035","q":"How does tool (function) calling work between a language model and an application?","a":"<p>You give the model a list of tools with names, descriptions and parameter schemas. The model does not run anything. It returns a structured request naming a tool and its arguments. Your application validates it, runs the tool, and returns the result to the model as a message. The loop repeats until the model answers without a tool request.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"agentic-ai-f0120","card":"q-agentic-ai-034-reflection-based-and-tool-integrated","sec":"agentic-ai-s035","q":"Why should tools that agents call be idempotent or protected against repeated calls?","a":"<p>Agents retry after errors or timeouts and may call the same tool twice. A non-idempotent action such as sending a payment or an email would then happen twice. Use idempotency keys, check state before acting, and require approval for irreversible actions.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"agentic-ai-f0121","card":"q-agentic-ai-035-planning-centric-gains-and-human-ai-coll","sec":"agentic-ai-s036","q":"What is a planning-centric agent, and why would you use one?","a":"<p>It breaks a complex task into manageable steps, plans them, and adapts during execution. It suits long tasks with dependencies, such as a multi-step upgrade, where acting without a plan would be inefficient or error prone. The trade-off is planning overhead and the risk of a plan going stale.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"agentic-ai-f0122","card":"q-agentic-ai-035-planning-centric-gains-and-human-ai-coll","sec":"agentic-ai-s036","q":"What is the best division of work in a human-AI collaboration architecture?","a":"<p>The AI handles routine, repetitive and high-volume tasks. Humans handle creativity, judgment, ethical decisions and exceptions. Design explicit handoff points and let humans override.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"agentic-ai-f0123","card":"q-agentic-ai-035-planning-centric-gains-and-human-ai-coll","sec":"agentic-ai-s036","q":"Why might you combine steps in a planning-centric agent?","a":"<p>Merging related steps reduces the number of operations and round trips, which saves time and cost. The plan must still keep dependencies and safety checks intact.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"agentic-ai-f0124","card":"q-agentic-ai-036-three-minor-architectures","sec":"agentic-ai-s037","q":"What is swarm intelligence, and when is it a good fit?","a":"<p>Many simple agents follow simple local rules, and useful collective behavior emerges, as in ant colonies or fish schools. It fits problems such as routing, search and coordinated robots, where robustness and scalability matter and no central controller is wanted. Its behavior can be hard to predict and tune.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"agentic-ai-f0125","card":"q-agentic-ai-036-three-minor-architectures","sec":"agentic-ai-s037","q":"What is a behavior-based architecture, and how is it related to reactive agents?","a":"<p>It organizes the agent as a set of specific behaviors triggered by the situation, closely related to reactive architectures. It responds fast and is simple, but it is not general across domains and does not plan far ahead.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"agentic-ai-f0126","card":"q-agentic-ai-036-three-minor-architectures","sec":"agentic-ai-s037","q":"What does an emotion-based architecture add, and what is the concern?","a":"<p>It simulates emotional responses such as empathy or anger to make interaction more natural and human-like. The concern is that simulated emotion can mislead or manipulate users, so it needs transparency and ethical limits.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"agentic-ai-f0127","card":"q-agentic-ai-037-six-pre-development-components-of-a-coop","sec":"agentic-ai-s038","q":"What six things should you define before building a cooperative multi-agent system?","a":"<p>The environment, communication protocols, shared goals and rewards, coordination mechanisms, decentralized decision-making, and role specialization.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"agentic-ai-f0128","card":"q-agentic-ai-037-six-pre-development-components-of-a-coop","sec":"agentic-ai-s038","q":"Why do cooperative agents need shared goals and aligned rewards?","a":"<p>If each agent optimizes only its own reward, they may compete or undermine each other. A shared structure, such as maximizing sales while minimizing costs, aligns individual behavior with the team's objective.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"agentic-ai-f0129","card":"q-agentic-ai-037-six-pre-development-components-of-a-coop","sec":"agentic-ai-s038","q":"What coordination mechanisms can you use in a multi-agent system?","a":"<p>Leader-follower structures, where one agent assigns work, and consensus-driven approaches, where agents agree on a decision. A leader is simple and fast but a single point of failure. Consensus is more resilient but slower.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"agentic-ai-f0130","card":"q-agentic-ai-037-six-pre-development-components-of-a-coop","sec":"agentic-ai-s038","q":"Why use role specialization in a multi-agent system?","a":"<p>Each agent focuses on what it does best, for example one handles customer chat and another processes orders. This improves quality and makes agents easier to build, test and replace.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"agentic-ai-f0131","card":"q-agentic-ai-038-why-decentralized-decision-making","sec":"agentic-ai-s039","q":"What is the benefit of decentralized decision-making in a multi-agent system?","a":"<p>There is no single point of failure. If one agent loses communication, it keeps working with local information while the others continue, which adds redundancy and resilience.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"agentic-ai-f0132","card":"q-agentic-ai-038-why-decentralized-decision-making","sec":"agentic-ai-s039","q":"What are the drawbacks of decentralized decision-making compared with a central controller?","a":"<p>Agents act on partial information, so decisions can conflict or be globally suboptimal. Consistency is harder, and coordination needs extra protocols. A central controller is easier to reason about but is a bottleneck and a single point of failure.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"agentic-ai-f0133","card":"q-agentic-ai-038-why-decentralized-decision-making","sec":"agentic-ai-s039","q":"How should an agent behave when it loses contact with the rest of the system?","a":"<p>It should fall back to safe local behavior based on its own information and its goals, keep a record of what it did, and resynchronize when the connection returns. Safety-critical actions should have conservative defaults.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"agentic-ai-f0134","card":"q-agentic-ai-039-communication-protocols","sec":"agentic-ai-s040","q":"Why do agents need communication protocols?","a":"<p>Without them agents misunderstand each other, spread shared errors and expose data to security breaches. Protocols give common rules for formats, meaning and security.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"agentic-ai-f0135","card":"q-agentic-ai-039-communication-protocols","sec":"agentic-ai-s040","q":"What are the elements of a communication protocol between agents?","a":"<p>Standardization (common rules), message formats (an agreed data structure such as JSON or XML), ontologies (a shared vocabulary such as one written in OWL), and security (SSL/TLS, OAuth, digital certificates).</p>","hand":true},{"id":"agentic-ai-f0136","card":"q-agentic-ai-039-communication-protocols","sec":"agentic-ai-s040","q":"What is an ontology in agent communication, and why is it needed?","a":"<p>An ontology is a shared, formal vocabulary of terms and their relationships. It ensures that two agents mean the same thing when they use a word. Without it, a field name or concept can be interpreted differently by each agent.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"agentic-ai-f0137","card":"q-agentic-ai-039-communication-protocols","sec":"agentic-ai-s040","q":"Why choose JSON over XML for agent messages, and when might you choose XML?","a":"<p>JSON is lighter, easier to read and parse, and native to most web APIs. XML supports schemas, namespaces and mature validation tooling, which can suit formal enterprise or standards-driven exchanges.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"agentic-ai-f0138","card":"q-agentic-ai-039-communication-protocols","sec":"agentic-ai-s040","q":"Why should agent messages follow a schema and be validated on receipt?","a":"<p>A malformed or unexpected message can crash an agent or trigger a wrong action. Validating against a schema catches errors early and blocks malicious input.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"agentic-ai-f0139","card":"q-agentic-ai-040-message-format-example","sec":"agentic-ai-s041","q":"In an agent-to-agent JSON message, what is the job of a <code>message_type</code> field such as <code>\"alert\"</code>?","a":"<p>It tells the receiver how to route and handle the message (alert, request, response, heartbeat and so on) before it parses the payload. Without it, every receiver would have to guess the intent from the payload shape. Keep the allowed values in a fixed list so all agents agree on them.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">JSON</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>{\n  &quot;agent_id&quot;: &quot;camera_01&quot;,\n  &quot;message_type&quot;: &quot;alert&quot;,\n  &quot;timestamp&quot;: &quot;2024-01-15T10:30:00Z&quot;,\n  &quot;payload&quot;: {\n    &quot;event&quot;: &quot;motion_detected&quot;,\n    &quot;confidence&quot;: 0.92,\n    &quot;action_required&quot;: true\n  }\n}</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"agentic-ai-f0140","card":"q-agentic-ai-040-message-format-example","sec":"agentic-ai-s041","q":"In an agent message, why keep envelope fields (sender id, type, timestamp) separate from a nested <code>payload</code> object?","a":"<p>The envelope has the same shape for every message, so routers, loggers and validators can handle it without knowing the business content. The payload changes per message type and can evolve on its own. This separation lets you add new payload types without changing the routing code.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"agentic-ai-f0141","card":"q-agentic-ai-040-message-format-example","sec":"agentic-ai-s041","q":"Why should an agent message carry a timestamp in ISO 8601 UTC form, such as <code>2024-01-15T10:30:00Z</code>?","a":"<p>Agents run on different machines and time zones. A UTC timestamp in a standard format is unambiguous, sorts correctly as text, and lets receivers order events, discard stale messages, detect replays and correlate logs. Local times without a zone cause wrong ordering and hard-to-trace bugs.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"agentic-ai-f0142","card":"q-agentic-ai-040-message-format-example","sec":"agentic-ai-s041","q":"In a message where the payload includes <code>\"confidence\": 0.92</code>, why send a confidence score along with the detected event?","a":"<p>The receiving agent can decide how to act on uncertainty: act at once when the score passes a threshold, ask for confirmation in the middle, or ignore low scores. Sending only the label hides how reliable it is. The scale (0 to 1) and meaning must be agreed in the protocol, or agents will misread it.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"agentic-ai-f0143","card":"q-agentic-ai-040-message-format-example","sec":"agentic-ai-s041","q":"What can go wrong if an agent receives a message with <code>\"action_required\": true</code> and acts on it without checks?","a":"<p>A forged, replayed or buggy message can trigger real actions. The receiver should authenticate the sender, validate the message against the schema, check the timestamp for freshness, and apply its own policy limits before acting. A flag in a message is a request, not an authority.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"agentic-ai-f0144","card":"q-agentic-ai-040-message-format-example","sec":"agentic-ai-s041","q":"How would you make agent messages safe to evolve over time as fields are added?","a":"<p>Add a version field (or schema version), make new fields optional, never change the meaning of an existing field, and have receivers ignore unknown fields. Remove fields only after all consumers have moved. This keeps old and new agents working together during a rolling upgrade.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"agentic-ai-f0145","card":"q-agentic-ai-040-message-format-example","sec":"agentic-ai-s041","q":"Why add a unique message id and correlation id to agent messages?","a":"<p>A message id lets receivers detect duplicates when delivery is retried (at-least-once delivery) and make handling idempotent. A correlation id links a response to the request that caused it and lets you trace one task across several agents in logs.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"agentic-ai-f0146","card":"q-agentic-ai-041-security-protocols-for-agent-communicati","sec":"agentic-ai-s042","q":"What does TLS protect in agent-to-agent communication, and what does it not protect?","a":"<p>TLS encrypts data in transit and lets the client verify the server's identity, so eavesdroppers and tampering on the network are blocked. It does not protect data at rest, does not stop a legitimate but compromised agent from sending bad messages, and does not decide what an agent is allowed to do.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"agentic-ai-f0147","card":"q-agentic-ai-041-security-protocols-for-agent-communicati","sec":"agentic-ai-s042","q":"Is OAuth an authentication protocol or an authorization framework?","a":"<p>OAuth 2.0 is an authorization framework: it lets a client obtain a scoped, time-limited access token to call an API. Authentication of the user is added by OpenID Connect on top of it. For agents, OAuth is typically used so an agent gets only the scopes it needs, using a client-credentials flow for machine-to-machine calls.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"agentic-ai-f0148","card":"q-agentic-ai-041-security-protocols-for-agent-communicati","sec":"agentic-ai-s042","q":"What problem do digital certificates solve between agents on a network?","a":"<p>They bind a public key to an identity, signed by a trusted certificate authority. A receiving agent can verify who it is talking to and does not have to trust a claimed name. Without them, an attacker could impersonate an agent (man-in-the-middle).</p>","hand":true},{"id":"agentic-ai-f0149","card":"q-agentic-ai-041-security-protocols-for-agent-communicati","sec":"agentic-ai-s042","q":"What is mutual TLS (mTLS), and why use it for agents?","a":"<p>In mTLS both sides present certificates, so the server also verifies the client. For agents, this proves each agent's identity in both directions and is a common base for zero-trust service-to-service traffic. The cost is certificate issuance, rotation and revocation work.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"agentic-ai-f0150","card":"q-agentic-ai-041-security-protocols-for-agent-communicati","sec":"agentic-ai-s042","q":"Why use short-lived OAuth access tokens for agents instead of long-lived static API keys?","a":"<p>If a token leaks, it expires soon, and it can be limited to specific scopes. A static key stays valid until someone rotates it and usually has broad rights. Short-lived tokens also support revocation and audit by client identity.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"agentic-ai-f0151","card":"q-agentic-ai-041-security-protocols-for-agent-communicati","sec":"agentic-ai-s042","q":"What is the principle of least privilege for an agent's credentials, and why does it matter more for autonomous agents?","a":"<p>Give each agent only the permissions needed for its task. An autonomous agent acts without a human reviewing each step, so a compromised or misled agent with broad rights can do widespread damage quickly. Narrow scopes limit that blast radius.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"agentic-ai-f0152","card":"q-agentic-ai-041-security-protocols-for-agent-communicati","sec":"agentic-ai-s042","q":"An agent receives a message over a valid TLS connection. Is it safe to act on it?","a":"<p>Not automatically. TLS only shows the channel is private and the peer was verified. You still need authorization (is this sender allowed to ask for this), input validation, replay protection, and policy checks. A trusted peer can still send malicious or wrong content, for example if it was compromised.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"agentic-ai-f0153","card":"q-agentic-ai-041-security-protocols-for-agent-communicati","sec":"agentic-ai-s042","q":"How do you protect agent messages from replay attacks?","a":"<p>Include a timestamp and a unique nonce or message id, reject messages outside a short time window, and remember recent ids. Signing the message, or using a channel that binds messages to a session, stops an attacker from altering these fields.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"agentic-ai-f0154","card":"q-agentic-ai-042-game-theory-in-agentic-ai","sec":"agentic-ai-s043","q":"Why is game theory relevant to multi-agent AI systems?","a":"<p>When agents' outcomes depend on each other's choices, each agent must reason about what others will do. Game theory gives a formal way to model players, strategies and payoffs, and to predict or design outcomes such as cooperation, competition or stable agreements. It is used for negotiation, resource allocation, auctions and security.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"agentic-ai-f0155","card":"q-agentic-ai-042-game-theory-in-agentic-ai","sec":"agentic-ai-s043","q":"In game theory, what is a payoff and why must it depend on all players' actions?","a":"<p>A payoff is the gain or loss a player receives from an outcome. It depends on the combined actions of all players because one agent's best choice changes with what the others do. If payoffs ignored the others, there would be no strategic interaction and a simple optimization would be enough.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"agentic-ai-f0156","card":"q-agentic-ai-042-game-theory-in-agentic-ai","sec":"agentic-ai-s043","q":"What is a Nash equilibrium?","a":"<p>A set of strategies where no player can improve their payoff by changing only their own strategy while the others keep theirs. It is a stable outcome, but not necessarily the best one for the group. The prisoner's dilemma is the classic case where the equilibrium is worse for both than cooperating.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"agentic-ai-f0157","card":"q-agentic-ai-042-game-theory-in-agentic-ai","sec":"agentic-ai-s043","q":"What does the prisoner's dilemma teach about designing multi-agent systems?","a":"<p>Individually rational agents can reach an outcome that is worse for everyone. To get cooperation, change the incentives: repeated interaction, reputation, penalties for defection, or shared rewards. Do not assume agents will cooperate just because it would be better overall.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"agentic-ai-f0158","card":"q-agentic-ai-042-game-theory-in-agentic-ai","sec":"agentic-ai-s043","q":"What assumptions of classic game theory often fail for real AI agents?","a":"<p>Agents may not be fully rational, may have incomplete or wrong information about others, may have unclear payoffs, and may learn and change over time. Real systems also have noise and communication limits. Game theory gives useful models, but results should be tested in simulation.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"agentic-ai-f0159","card":"q-agentic-ai-042-game-theory-in-agentic-ai","sec":"agentic-ai-s043","q":"What is a dominant strategy, and what does it say about a game's outcome?","a":"<p>A dominant strategy gives a player a payoff at least as good as any other choice, whatever the other players do. If every player has one, the outcome is predictable without guessing opponents' moves. In the prisoner's dilemma, defecting is dominant for both players.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"agentic-ai-f0160","card":"q-agentic-ai-042-game-theory-in-agentic-ai","sec":"agentic-ai-s043","q":"What is Pareto optimality, and why can a Nash equilibrium fail to be Pareto optimal?","a":"<p>An outcome is Pareto optimal if no player can be made better off without making someone worse off. A Nash equilibrium only needs each player to have no solo improvement, so players can be stuck in an outcome that all would prefer to leave together. The prisoner's dilemma is the standard example. To fix it, change incentives with repetition, penalties or binding agreements.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"agentic-ai-f0161","card":"q-agentic-ai-043-types-of-games","sec":"agentic-ai-s044","q":"What is the difference between a cooperative and a non-cooperative game in multi-agent systems?","a":"<p>In a cooperative game, agents can form binding alliances and share the joint gain, such as agents splitting work toward a common goal. In a non-cooperative game, each agent acts for its own benefit and no binding agreements exist, such as agents competing for limited compute or bandwidth.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"agentic-ai-f0162","card":"q-agentic-ai-043-types-of-games","sec":"agentic-ai-s044","q":"What is a zero-sum game, and how does it differ from a non-zero-sum game?","a":"<p>In a zero-sum game, one agent's gain is exactly another's loss, so the total is fixed (for example, splitting a fixed budget). In a non-zero-sum game the total can change, so all agents can win or lose together. Many real tasks are non-zero-sum, which is why cooperation can pay off.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"agentic-ai-f0163","card":"q-agentic-ai-043-types-of-games","sec":"agentic-ai-s044","q":"Why is it useful to frame a collaborative multi-agent task as non-zero-sum?","a":"<p>It lets you reward agents for joint results instead of making them fight over a fixed amount. Agents then have a reason to share information and help each other. Framing a shared problem as zero-sum creates needless competition and hurts the total outcome.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"agentic-ai-f0164","card":"q-agentic-ai-043-types-of-games","sec":"agentic-ai-s044","q":"Two agents share one fixed pool of GPU hours. Which game type is this, and what does it imply for design?","a":"<p>It is closer to a zero-sum, non-cooperative game: more for one means less for the other. A fair allocation rule, quotas, or an auction or arbiter is needed to prevent hoarding. If agents can trade or share unused time, the game becomes non-zero-sum and total value rises.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"agentic-ai-f0165","card":"q-agentic-ai-043-types-of-games","sec":"agentic-ai-s044","q":"Can a game be both cooperative and zero-sum?","a":"<p>Technically yes, but it adds nothing. Zero-sum means the total payoff is fixed, and cooperative means players can make binding agreements. With a fixed total there is no joint surplus to create, so cooperation cannot raise the overall result and only splits the fixed amount. Cooperation matters in non-zero-sum games.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"agentic-ai-f0166","card":"q-agentic-ai-044-agent-strategies","sec":"agentic-ai-s045","q":"What is the difference between a deterministic and a randomized strategy for an agent?","a":"<p>A deterministic strategy picks the same action for a given state, so it is predictable and easy to test. A randomized strategy picks among actions by chance, so an opponent cannot predict it. Use deterministic rules for safety and repeatability, and randomization against adversaries who could exploit a pattern.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"agentic-ai-f0167","card":"q-agentic-ai-044-agent-strategies","sec":"agentic-ai-s045","q":"What is a mixed strategy in game theory, and when would an agent randomize its actions?","a":"<p>A mixed strategy assigns a probability to each action and samples from it. Randomize when a predictable choice can be exploited, such as against an adaptive opponent. At a mixed equilibrium each player's probabilities make the other indifferent between its own actions, so neither can gain by deviating and the pattern cannot be exploited. It also supports exploration while learning.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"agentic-ai-f0168","card":"q-agentic-ai-044-agent-strategies","sec":"agentic-ai-s045","q":"Why is a fixed strategy a weakness against an adaptive opponent?","a":"<p>Once the opponent learns the pattern, it can respond with the best counter and win every time. A fixed strategy is only fine when the environment or opponent is not adaptive. Against adaptive agents, use state-dependent or randomized strategies.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"agentic-ai-f0169","card":"q-agentic-ai-044-agent-strategies","sec":"agentic-ai-s045","q":"Why would you use a deterministic strategy for a safety-critical agent instead of a randomized one?","a":"<p>Behavior must be predictable, explainable and testable, and the same input must give the same action for audit and certification. Randomness makes failures hard to reproduce. Randomization is acceptable only where the downside of an unlucky choice is small.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"agentic-ai-f0170","card":"q-agentic-ai-044-agent-strategies","sec":"agentic-ai-s045","q":"How do you choose between fixed, deterministic, randomized and probabilistic strategies?","a":"<p>Ask whether the environment is adversarial, whether predictability is a risk or a requirement, and how much is known about the others. Fixed fits simple, stable settings. Deterministic fits rule-driven, auditable behavior. Randomized or probabilistic fits competitive settings or exploration under uncertainty.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"agentic-ai-f0171","card":"q-agentic-ai-045-negotiation-protocols","sec":"agentic-ai-s046","q":"What is a negotiation protocol in a multi-agent system, and why is one needed?","a":"<p>It is a set of rules for the interaction: who can propose, what message types exist, how counter-offers work, when the process ends. Without it, agents have no shared way to reach consensus and negotiations can loop or deadlock. It makes the outcome predictable and auditable.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"agentic-ai-f0172","card":"q-agentic-ai-045-negotiation-protocols","sec":"agentic-ai-s046","q":"In agent negotiation, what should happen when the agents cannot reach agreement?","a":"<p>Escalate: add a human or a designated arbiter agent to decide, or fall back to a default rule. Also set a limit on rounds or time so negotiation cannot continue forever. Escalation paths should be part of the protocol design.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"agentic-ai-f0173","card":"q-agentic-ai-045-negotiation-protocols","sec":"agentic-ai-s046","q":"Why put a maximum number of rounds or a deadline in a negotiation protocol?","a":"<p>Agents might keep making small concessions or counter-offers indefinitely, wasting resources and delaying decisions. A limit guarantees termination. After the limit, the protocol uses a default outcome or escalation.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"agentic-ai-f0174","card":"q-agentic-ai-045-negotiation-protocols","sec":"agentic-ai-s046","q":"What are common negotiation mechanisms between agents?","a":"<p>Direct bilateral bargaining (offers and counter-offers), auctions (English, Dutch, sealed-bid), contract-net (a manager announces a task, agents bid, the manager awards it), and voting for group choices. Choose by number of parties, whether there is a central coordinator, and whether price discovery is needed.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"agentic-ai-f0175","card":"q-agentic-ai-045-negotiation-protocols","sec":"agentic-ai-s046","q":"How does the contract-net protocol work?","a":"<p>A manager agent broadcasts a task announcement. Capable agents reply with bids. The manager evaluates the bids and awards the task to the best one, and the winner reports the result. It distributes work dynamically without the manager knowing each agent's capacity in advance.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"agentic-ai-f0176","card":"q-agentic-ai-046-utility-functions-in-negotiation","sec":"agentic-ai-s047","q":"What role does a utility function play when an agent negotiates?","a":"<p>It scores each possible outcome numerically so the agent can compare offers, decide what to accept, and find trade-offs. Without it, the agent has no consistent way to say one deal is better than another. It can also be used to state the agent's goals to the other side.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"agentic-ai-f0177","card":"q-agentic-ai-046-utility-functions-in-negotiation","sec":"agentic-ai-s047","q":"How can an agent use its utility function to decide whether to accept an offer?","a":"<p>Compare the offer's utility with its reservation value, the utility of the best alternative if no deal is reached. Accept if the offer is at least as good, otherwise counter-propose or walk away. The best alternative to a negotiated agreement sets the floor.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"agentic-ai-f0178","card":"q-agentic-ai-046-utility-functions-in-negotiation","sec":"agentic-ai-s047","q":"Why should an agent not reveal its full utility function to the other negotiating agents?","a":"<p>The other side could use it to extract the maximum concession, such as offering exactly the lowest value the agent will accept. Agents should share goals and constraints needed to reach agreement but keep reservation values private, unless the setting is fully cooperative.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"agentic-ai-f0179","card":"q-agentic-ai-046-utility-functions-in-negotiation","sec":"agentic-ai-s047","q":"A pricing agent has predefined best outcomes. How does a utility function help it find a deal with a buyer agent?","a":"<p>It scores combinations of price, quantity and delivery time, so the agent can propose offers with high utility for itself that still look acceptable to the buyer. It can trade a lower price for a larger quantity if that raises its total utility. This turns a vague aim into searchable options.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"agentic-ai-f0180","card":"q-agentic-ai-046-utility-functions-in-negotiation","sec":"agentic-ai-s047","q":"What goes wrong if two negotiating agents have utility functions that weigh different attributes without any shared scale?","a":"<p>Offers cannot be compared fairly and agents may misread each other's goals. Agree on the attributes being negotiated and their meaning in the protocol. Each agent can still weigh them differently internally, which is what makes mutually good trades possible.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"agentic-ai-f0181","card":"q-agentic-ai-047-conflict-resolution-principles","sec":"agentic-ai-s048","q":"What principles make conflict resolution between agents work?","a":"<p>Shared end goals, a defined list of prohibited actions, fairness rules, a way to learn from past conflicts, and analysis of negative outcomes to improve later negotiations. Together they give agents a common basis to agree and a boundary they cannot cross.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"agentic-ai-f0182","card":"q-agentic-ai-047-conflict-resolution-principles","sec":"agentic-ai-s048","q":"Why does a shared end goal matter when resolving agent conflicts?","a":"<p>It gives agents a common test for judging proposals. Conflicts are then about how to reach the goal, and compromise is possible. Without it, each agent optimizes for itself and there is nothing to settle against.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"agentic-ai-f0183","card":"q-agentic-ai-047-conflict-resolution-principles","sec":"agentic-ai-s048","q":"Why model fairness in agent conflict resolution on human legal and institutional practice?","a":"<p>Humans have long-tested mechanisms (due process, equal treatment, proportional remedies, appeals) that people accept as legitimate. Reusing them gives agents resolution rules that stakeholders can understand and audit. Invented rules can look arbitrary and be exploited.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"agentic-ai-f0184","card":"q-agentic-ai-047-conflict-resolution-principles","sec":"agentic-ai-s048","q":"How do you design a conflict-resolution flow when two agents want the same limited resource?","a":"<p>Define a priority or fairness rule up front (priority, queue, auction, quota). Let agents negotiate within it, set a timeout, and escalate to an arbiter or human when they deadlock. Log every decision for audit and tune the rule from past outcomes.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"agentic-ai-f0185","card":"q-agentic-ai-048-agent-learning-from-conflict","sec":"agentic-ai-s049","q":"How can an agent improve its negotiation after a conflict is resolved?","a":"<p>It records the interaction and outcome, finds where it lost value or caused a deadlock, and adjusts its strategy, for example through reinforcement learning, updated utility weights or better opening offers. Past negative outcomes become training data for future negotiations.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"agentic-ai-f0186","card":"q-agentic-ai-048-agent-learning-from-conflict","sec":"agentic-ai-s049","q":"What are the risks when agents learn from conflicts automatically?","a":"<p>They may learn to exploit others, drift toward unfair strategies, overfit to one counterpart, or learn from biased or manipulated outcomes. Monitor their behavior over time.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"agentic-ai-f0187","card":"q-agentic-ai-048-agent-learning-from-conflict","sec":"agentic-ai-s049","q":"Why should learned negotiation updates be validated before use in production?","a":"<p>A bad update can change behavior in ways nobody expects, with real cost in deals or safety. Test in simulation or on a small share of traffic, compare against the current policy, and keep rollback ready. Learning is useful only when it is controlled.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"agentic-ai-f0188","card":"q-agentic-ai-049-why-ethics-matters","sec":"agentic-ai-s050","q":"Why must ethics be considered at every stage of an agentic AI system, not only at deployment?","a":"<p>Design choices (data, objectives, tools) create bias and risk long before release, and later fixes are costly. Ethics checks belong in design, build, deployment and operations. An agent acts autonomously, so errors scale quickly and affect real people.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"agentic-ai-f0189","card":"q-agentic-ai-049-why-ethics-matters","sec":"agentic-ai-s050","q":"What are the business and legal consequences of a biased AI decision, such as a loan system rejecting applicants by race?","a":"<p>Illegal discrimination under laws such as fair lending and equal opportunity rules, fines, lawsuits, reputational damage and loss of customer trust, besides the moral harm. The organization is accountable even if the model made the decision.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"agentic-ai-f0190","card":"q-agentic-ai-049-why-ethics-matters","sec":"agentic-ai-s050","q":"Why do autonomous agents raise ethical risk more than ordinary software?","a":"<p>They decide and act on their own, often at scale and with unclear internal reasoning, so a flaw can cause many harmful actions before anyone notices. They also learn and change over time, so behavior seen at launch may not match later behavior.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"agentic-ai-f0191","card":"q-agentic-ai-050-six-ethical-framework-components","sec":"agentic-ai-s051","q":"What are the components of an ethical framework for agentic AI?","a":"<p>Fairness, transparency, explainability, accountability, privacy and data protection. Fairness limits bias, transparency and explainability make decisions understandable, accountability assigns responsibility, and privacy and data protection control personal data.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"agentic-ai-f0192","card":"q-agentic-ai-050-six-ethical-framework-components","sec":"agentic-ai-s051","q":"What is the difference between transparency and explainability in AI?","a":"<p>Transparency is about openness of the system: how it was built, what data it uses, and its overall logic. Explainability is about being able to explain a specific decision in terms a person understands. A system can be transparent in design yet give no useful explanation of one output.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"agentic-ai-f0193","card":"q-agentic-ai-050-six-ethical-framework-components","sec":"agentic-ai-s051","q":"What is the difference between privacy and data protection?","a":"<p>Privacy is about limiting what personal data is collected and how it is used (minimization, consent, anonymization). Data protection is about keeping that data secure from unauthorized access or leaks (encryption, access control). You need both: collecting less data and protecting what you hold.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"agentic-ai-f0194","card":"q-agentic-ai-050-six-ethical-framework-components","sec":"agentic-ai-s051","q":"How would you turn the ethical framework components into engineering practice?","a":"<p>Fairness: bias testing on subgroups. Transparency and explainability: documentation, model cards, explanation methods. Accountability: audit logs, owners, review boards. Privacy and data protection: data minimization, anonymization, encryption and access control. Check each at release gates.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"agentic-ai-f0195","card":"q-agentic-ai-051-fairness-frameworks","sec":"agentic-ai-s052","q":"How do you reduce bias in an AI agent's training data?","a":"<p>Use diverse, representative data across groups, audit it for imbalance and proxy features, rebalance or reweight where needed, and test model outcomes by subgroup. Also review labels, since human labelers can add bias. Repeat after deployment because data shifts.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"agentic-ai-f0196","card":"q-agentic-ai-051-fairness-frameworks","sec":"agentic-ai-s052","q":"Why does removing a protected attribute such as race or gender from the data not guarantee fairness?","a":"<p>Other features (zip code, school, purchase history) can act as proxies and carry the same information. The model can still discriminate. You need to test outcomes by group and check proxy features, not just drop columns.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"agentic-ai-f0197","card":"q-agentic-ai-051-fairness-frameworks","sec":"agentic-ai-s052","q":"Name common ways to measure fairness in a model's decisions.","a":"<p>Demographic parity (similar positive rates across groups), equal opportunity (similar true positive rates), and equalized odds (similar true and false positive rates). These can conflict with each other and with accuracy, so choose the metric that matches the harm you want to prevent.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"agentic-ai-f0198","card":"q-agentic-ai-051-fairness-frameworks","sec":"agentic-ai-s052","q":"What are the stages where bias can be mitigated in an ML pipeline?","a":"<p>Pre-processing (fix or reweight the data), in-processing (add fairness constraints to training), and post-processing (adjust decisions or thresholds after prediction). Pre-processing is usually the cheapest to start with. Monitor in production because bias can reappear.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"agentic-ai-f0199","card":"q-agentic-ai-052-transparency-and-explainability","sec":"agentic-ai-s053","q":"Why does explainability build trust in an agent's decisions?","a":"<p>Users, auditors and regulators can see why a decision was made, challenge it, and spot errors or bias. It also helps developers debug. Without explanations, people must accept results on faith, which is risky in finance, healthcare and law.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"agentic-ai-f0200","card":"q-agentic-ai-052-transparency-and-explainability","sec":"agentic-ai-s053","q":"What does \"trust but verify\" mean for AI systems?","a":"<p>Stakeholders can rely on the agent's output but must be able to check how it got there. That needs audit trails, logged inputs, decisions and reasons, and the ability to reproduce or review a decision. Trust is conditional on verification.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"agentic-ai-f0201","card":"q-agentic-ai-052-transparency-and-explainability","sec":"agentic-ai-s053","q":"What are the trade-offs between interpretable models and complex black-box models?","a":"<p>Simple models such as linear models and decision trees are easy to explain but may be less accurate on hard tasks. Deep models are often more accurate but opaque, so you need post-hoc tools (SHAP, LIME, attention analysis). In regulated settings, favour the simplest model that meets the accuracy need.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"agentic-ai-f0202","card":"q-agentic-ai-052-transparency-and-explainability","sec":"agentic-ai-s053","q":"What is an audit trail for an AI agent, and what should it record?","a":"<p>A tamper-resistant record of what the agent did and why. Record inputs, tool calls, the decision and its reasoning or score, model and policy versions, timestamp, and any human approval. This supports debugging, compliance reviews and incident analysis.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"agentic-ai-f0203","card":"q-agentic-ai-052-transparency-and-explainability","sec":"agentic-ai-s053","q":"What are SHAP and LIME used for?","a":"<p>They are post-hoc explanation methods that estimate how much each input feature contributed to a specific prediction. They make black-box models more explainable, but the explanations are approximations and can be unstable, so they support but do not replace good model design.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"agentic-ai-f0204","card":"q-agentic-ai-053-accountability","sec":"agentic-ai-s054","q":"What does accountability mean for an organization that runs autonomous agents?","a":"<p>Someone is clearly responsible for the agent's behavior, with governance policies, audit trails and defined liability for harm or errors. Developers and operators must stay within legal and ethical limits, and the system must track itself so problems are found early.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"agentic-ai-f0205","card":"q-agentic-ai-053-accountability","sec":"agentic-ai-s054","q":"Why define liability for AI errors before deployment?","a":"<p>When an agent causes harm, disputes about who pays or who fixes it are slow and costly. Agree ahead of time on responsibility among developer, operator and user, and put it in contracts and governance policies. It also forces teams to assess risk before launch.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"agentic-ai-f0206","card":"q-agentic-ai-053-accountability","sec":"agentic-ai-s054","q":"What governance mechanisms help keep AI agents accountable?","a":"<p>Named owners, an approval process for changes, risk assessments, audit logs, monitoring and incident response, periodic reviews, and the right to appeal or have a human review decisions. Together they make agent behavior traceable and correctable.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"agentic-ai-f0207","card":"q-agentic-ai-053-accountability","sec":"agentic-ai-s054","q":"Why should a system monitor itself for issues instead of waiting for users to report them?","a":"<p>Agents can fail silently or drift without any visible error. Automated monitoring, alerts and logs find problems early, limit harm and fines, and give evidence for accountability. Users report only what they notice.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"agentic-ai-f0208","card":"q-agentic-ai-054-privacy-and-data-protection","sec":"agentic-ai-s055","q":"What is data minimization, and why does it matter for agents?","a":"<p>Collect and keep only the data needed for the task. Data you never collect cannot leak, be misused or be subpoenaed, and it lowers compliance burden under laws such as GDPR. Agents often have access to many tools and records, so limit what they can read.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"agentic-ai-f0209","card":"q-agentic-ai-054-privacy-and-data-protection","sec":"agentic-ai-s055","q":"What is the difference between anonymization and pseudonymization?","a":"<p>Anonymization removes identifiers so data can no longer be linked back to a person, and it is then generally outside privacy law. Pseudonymization replaces identifiers with tokens, but a key can re-link the data, so it is still personal data. Anonymization can fail through re-identification when other datasets are combined.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"agentic-ai-f0210","card":"q-agentic-ai-054-privacy-and-data-protection","sec":"agentic-ai-s055","q":"How can an agent leak personal data, and how do you prevent it?","a":"<p>Through logs, prompts sent to external services, tool outputs, memory, and answers that include data from other users. Prevent with data minimization, redaction before sending to external models, access control scoped to the user, encryption, and output filtering. Test for leakage.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"agentic-ai-f0211","card":"q-agentic-ai-054-privacy-and-data-protection","sec":"agentic-ai-s055","q":"Why strip names and IDs from medical records before using them to train an agent?","a":"<p>Medical records are highly sensitive and regulated (for example HIPAA in the US). Removing direct identifiers reduces the risk and harm if data leaks, and allows broader use for training. Quasi-identifiers such as birth date and zip code must also be handled to avoid re-identification.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"agentic-ai-f0212","card":"q-agentic-ai-054-privacy-and-data-protection","sec":"agentic-ai-s055","q":"What does GDPR require that affects agentic AI systems?","a":"<p>Lawful basis and consent for processing, purpose limitation, data minimization, rights to access and erasure, and rights around solely automated decisions with significant effect, including human review and meaningful explanations. Operators must be able to show compliance.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"agentic-ai-f0213","card":"q-agentic-ai-055-ethical-design-checklist","sec":"agentic-ai-s056","q":"What should you check before releasing an autonomous agent from an ethics point of view?","a":"<p>Bias tested across groups and datasets diverse, decision logic documented and explainable, governance and audit trails in place with owners named, personal data minimized and anonymized where possible, and data protected. Also confirm human oversight and a way to stop the agent.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"agentic-ai-f0214","card":"q-agentic-ai-055-ethical-design-checklist","sec":"agentic-ai-s056","q":"How would you handle a release where fairness testing shows worse outcomes for one group?","a":"<p>Block or limit the release, find the cause (data imbalance, proxy features, thresholds), fix and retest, and document the trade-offs. If a residual gap remains, add human review for affected cases and monitor in production.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"agentic-ai-f0215","card":"q-agentic-ai-056-jobs-regulation-and-cybersecurity","sec":"agentic-ai-s057","q":"How should organizations handle job displacement caused by agentic AI?","a":"<p>Plan the transition: retrain and reskill employees, move people to roles that use judgment and oversight of agents, communicate early, and track impact. Automation also creates new roles, but the shift needs deliberate management.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"agentic-ai-f0216","card":"q-agentic-ai-056-jobs-regulation-and-cybersecurity","sec":"agentic-ai-s057","q":"Why do regulatory frameworks struggle to keep up with agentic AI?","a":"<p>The technology changes faster than law is written, behavior is hard to predict, and rules differ between countries. Regulators tend to use risk-based approaches such as the EU AI Act, which sets stricter duties for high-risk uses. Organizations should build governance that adapts.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"agentic-ai-f0217","card":"q-agentic-ai-056-jobs-regulation-and-cybersecurity","sec":"agentic-ai-s057","q":"What is the legal personhood debate about AI agents?","a":"<p>Agents act like employees with roles and independent decisions, so some ask whether they should have legal status or liability. Currently, responsibility stays with humans and organizations. The debate is about how to assign liability when an agent acts autonomously.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"agentic-ai-f0218","card":"q-agentic-ai-056-jobs-regulation-and-cybersecurity","sec":"agentic-ai-s057","q":"What does \"AI agents must be trusted entities\" mean in security terms?","a":"<p>Agents must be treated like privileged identities: authenticated, given least-privilege access, monitored and auditable. They should not become a route for attackers or leak protected data. Trust is earned through controls, not assumed.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"agentic-ai-f0219","card":"q-agentic-ai-056-jobs-regulation-and-cybersecurity","sec":"agentic-ai-s057","q":"How can bad actors take control of an agent, and how do you prevent it?","a":"<p>Through stolen credentials, prompt injection, compromised tools or data, or insecure APIs. Prevent with strong authentication, least privilege, input validation, sandboxing of tools, approval for risky actions, and monitoring for unusual behavior.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"agentic-ai-f0220","card":"q-agentic-ai-057-four-security-challenges","sec":"agentic-ai-s058","q":"What are the main security challenges specific to agentic AI?","a":"<p>Unpredictable inputs from multi-step interactions, complex and opaque decision-making, diverse environments the agent was not trained for, and untrusted external entities such as other systems, people and data. Each widens the attack surface or makes problems harder to find.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"agentic-ai-f0221","card":"q-agentic-ai-057-four-security-challenges","sec":"agentic-ai-s058","q":"Why are agents harder to secure than conventional software?","a":"<p>Behavior depends on open-ended inputs and a learned model, so you cannot enumerate every path and test it. They also call tools and act autonomously, so a failure has real effects. Conventional input validation and tests are necessary but not sufficient.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"agentic-ai-f0222","card":"q-agentic-ai-057-four-security-challenges","sec":"agentic-ai-s058","q":"How would you approach security for an agent when perfect security is impossible?","a":"<p>Understand the threats first, then reduce risk in layers: least privilege, input validation, sandboxing, human approval for high-impact actions, monitoring and incident response. Aim to limit the blast radius, not to remove every risk.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"agentic-ai-f0223","card":"q-agentic-ai-057-four-security-challenges","sec":"agentic-ai-s058","q":"Why do new deployment environments create new security risks for an agent?","a":"<p>The agent meets inputs, systems and users it was not tested against, so behavior and failure modes are unknown. Controls assumed to exist may be missing. Re-assess risks and test again in each new environment before wide release.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"agentic-ai-f0224","card":"q-agentic-ai-058-unpredictable-inputs-in-depth","sec":"agentic-ai-s059","q":"A customer splits a payment across two credit cards and PayPal, and an agent exposes personal data in that flow. What kind of problem is this, and how do you prevent similar ones?","a":"<p>It is an unforeseen input sequence, not an attack: no one designed or tested the multi-step path. Prevent it with threat modeling and abuse-case testing during development, fuzzing and scenario tests, strict output filtering of sensitive data, and fail-safe defaults when a flow is unknown.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"agentic-ai-f0225","card":"q-agentic-ai-058-unpredictable-inputs-in-depth","sec":"agentic-ai-s059","q":"Why is \"we did not foresee that scenario\" not an acceptable excuse for a security flaw in an agent?","a":"<p>Foreseeing unusual use is part of the job: regulators and customers expect risk assessment during design. You cannot list every case, but you can use threat modeling, red teaming and defaults that fail safe, and you are still accountable for the outcome.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"agentic-ai-f0226","card":"q-agentic-ai-058-unpredictable-inputs-in-depth","sec":"agentic-ai-s059","q":"How do you test an agent against unpredictable user inputs?","a":"<p>Build scenario libraries of multi-step and edge cases, use red teaming and adversarial prompts, run fuzzing and randomized simulations, and review production logs for new patterns. Add each discovered failure as a regression test.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"agentic-ai-f0227","card":"q-agentic-ai-058-unpredictable-inputs-in-depth","sec":"agentic-ai-s059","q":"What defaults should an agent apply when it meets an input flow it does not recognize?","a":"<p>Fail safe: refuse or ask for clarification, avoid exposing or moving sensitive data, hand off to a human for high-impact actions, and log the case for review. Do not guess when the downside is harm.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"agentic-ai-f0228","card":"q-agentic-ai-059-complex-and-opaque-decision-making","sec":"agentic-ai-s060","q":"Why does opaque decision-making create a security problem for AI agents?","a":"<p>If you cannot see why the agent produced an output, you cannot find the root cause of errors or find vulnerabilities such as poisoned data or prompt injection. Incident response is slower and attacks may go unnoticed.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"agentic-ai-f0229","card":"q-agentic-ai-059-complex-and-opaque-decision-making","sec":"agentic-ai-s060","q":"What helps you trace unexpected behavior in a complex agent?","a":"<p>Detailed logging of inputs, intermediate reasoning steps, tool calls and outputs, distributed tracing across agents, versioned models and prompts, replayable runs, and explanation tools. Observability is designed in before incidents occur.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"agentic-ai-f0230","card":"q-agentic-ai-059-complex-and-opaque-decision-making","sec":"agentic-ai-s060","q":"How does logging each tool call and decision of an agent help with security?","a":"<p>It gives an audit trail for investigating incidents, lets you detect anomalies such as unusual tool use, and supports rollback and compliance. Make logs tamper-resistant and avoid writing sensitive data into them.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"agentic-ai-f0231","card":"q-agentic-ai-060-untrusted-external-entities","sec":"agentic-ai-s061","q":"Why should an agent treat data from external systems, users and files as untrusted?","a":"<p>Any of them may be compromised, malicious or corrupted. Data can carry hidden instructions (prompt injection), wrong values or malware. Validate and sanitize input, limit what an agent does with it, and do not let external content change the agent's instructions.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"agentic-ai-f0232","card":"q-agentic-ai-060-untrusted-external-entities","sec":"agentic-ai-s061","q":"What is indirect prompt injection against an agent?","a":"<p>An attacker hides instructions in content the agent reads, such as a web page, email or document, and the agent follows them as if they came from the user. Mitigate by separating instructions from data, limiting tool permissions, requiring approval for sensitive actions, and filtering inputs and outputs.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"agentic-ai-f0233","card":"q-agentic-ai-060-untrusted-external-entities","sec":"agentic-ai-s061","q":"How do you limit damage if an agent is manipulated by untrusted data?","a":"<p>Least-privilege tool access, sandboxing, confirmation for irreversible actions, rate limits, output checks, and monitoring. Assume that manipulation will sometimes succeed and make sure the effect is small.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"agentic-ai-f0234","card":"q-agentic-ai-060-untrusted-external-entities","sec":"agentic-ai-s061","q":"How do you handle data that your own organization owns but may be corrupted?","a":"<p>Owned data is not automatically trustworthy. Use integrity checks (hashes, signatures), validation rules, provenance tracking, access control and backups, and monitor for anomalies. Corrupted training data also needs detection before it affects a model (data poisoning).</p>","hand":true},{"id":"agentic-ai-f0235","card":"q-agentic-ai-061-robustness-versus-fault-tolerance","sec":"agentic-ai-s062","q":"What is the difference between robustness and fault tolerance?","a":"<p>Robustness is correct behavior under diverse or unexpected conditions, such as unusual inputs or noise. Fault tolerance is continuing to operate when a component fails, such as a crashed agent or server. One handles bad conditions, the other handles broken parts.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"agentic-ai-f0236","card":"q-agentic-ai-061-robustness-versus-fault-tolerance","sec":"agentic-ai-s062","q":"Give an example of a system that is robust but not fault tolerant, and the reverse.","a":"<p>A model that handles noisy, varied inputs well but runs on one server that crashes is robust but not fault tolerant. A pair of replicated servers that fail over cleanly but give wrong answers on unusual inputs is fault tolerant but not robust.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"agentic-ai-f0237","card":"q-agentic-ai-061-robustness-versus-fault-tolerance","sec":"agentic-ai-s062","q":"How do you achieve fault tolerance in a multi-agent system?","a":"<p>Redundancy with automatic failover, health checks, retries with backoff, timeouts, circuit breakers, decentralized operation so one agent's loss does not stop the rest, and state stored outside the agent so another can resume.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"agentic-ai-f0238","card":"q-agentic-ai-061-robustness-versus-fault-tolerance","sec":"agentic-ai-s062","q":"How do you improve the robustness of an ML-based agent?","a":"<p>Train on diverse and noisy data, use data augmentation and adversarial testing, validate inputs, calibrate confidence and reject uncertain cases, and monitor for drift in production.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"agentic-ai-f0239","card":"q-agentic-ai-062-five-reliability-strategies","sec":"agentic-ai-s063","q":"What strategies improve the reliability of agentic AI systems?","a":"<p>Redundancy, error detection and correction, robust training data, system monitoring and diagnostics, and graceful degradation. They cover prevention (training data), detection (monitoring, error checks) and response (failover, degraded mode).</p>","hand":true},{"id":"agentic-ai-f0240","card":"q-agentic-ai-062-five-reliability-strategies","sec":"agentic-ai-s063","q":"How do the reliability strategies work together when a failure occurs?","a":"<p>Monitoring detects the problem, diagnostics find the cause, error correction fixes small faults automatically, redundancy takes over for failed components, and graceful degradation keeps minimum service if the full service cannot be restored. Robust training reduces how often failures happen.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"agentic-ai-f0241","card":"q-agentic-ai-062-five-reliability-strategies","sec":"agentic-ai-s063","q":"Which reliability strategy would you apply first for a new agent in production, and why?","a":"<p>Monitoring and diagnostics, because you cannot fix or improve what you cannot see, and it gives the data to decide which other strategies matter most. Then add redundancy for critical paths and graceful degradation for user-facing features.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"agentic-ai-f0242","card":"q-agentic-ai-063-redundancy","sec":"agentic-ai-s064","q":"What is redundancy in an agent system, and how does automatic failover work?","a":"<p>Run more than one instance of a critical agent so a backup can take over if the primary fails. Failover detects the failure (health check or heartbeat) and routes work to the backup without manual action. The result is higher availability.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"agentic-ai-f0243","card":"q-agentic-ai-063-redundancy","sec":"agentic-ai-s064","q":"What is the difference between active-active and active-passive redundancy?","a":"<p>In active-active, all instances handle traffic, so capacity is used and failover is fast. In active-passive, a standby waits idle until the primary fails, which is simpler and cheaper to keep consistent, but failover takes longer and the standby may be untested.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"agentic-ai-f0244","card":"q-agentic-ai-063-redundancy","sec":"agentic-ai-s064","q":"What are the costs and risks of redundancy?","a":"<p>Extra infrastructure cost, added complexity, and state synchronization problems. Two agents acting on the same task can produce duplicate actions or split-brain, so you need leader election, idempotent actions or locking. Redundant copies sharing the same bug or dependency also fail together.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"agentic-ai-f0245","card":"q-agentic-ai-063-redundancy","sec":"agentic-ai-s064","q":"How do you prevent duplicate actions when a backup agent takes over from a failed primary?","a":"<p>Make operations idempotent with unique request ids, store task state in shared durable storage, and use leases or leader election so only one agent acts at a time. The backup resumes from the last checkpoint.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"agentic-ai-f0246","card":"q-agentic-ai-064-error-detection-and-correction","sec":"agentic-ai-s065","q":"How can an agent detect and correct its own errors without human involvement?","a":"<p>Use validation rules, consistency checks, anomaly detection and confidence thresholds to detect errors, then apply a correction such as fixing a value from a trusted source, retrying, or asking another agent. Record the correction. For example, correcting an invalid zip code on an order before shipping.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"agentic-ai-f0247","card":"q-agentic-ai-064-error-detection-and-correction","sec":"agentic-ai-s065","q":"What is the risk of fully automatic error correction?","a":"<p>A wrong correction can hide the real problem or introduce new errors silently, such as changing a valid but unusual value. Limit automatic fixes to well-understood cases, log each change, and send low-confidence cases to a human.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"agentic-ai-f0248","card":"q-agentic-ai-064-error-detection-and-correction","sec":"agentic-ai-s065","q":"What is a checksum, and how does it relate to error detection in messages?","a":"<p>A checksum or hash is a small value computed from data. The receiver recomputes it and compares, so corrupted data is detected. Cryptographic hashes and signatures also detect tampering. Error-correcting codes go further and can repair small errors.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"agentic-ai-f0249","card":"q-agentic-ai-064-error-detection-and-correction","sec":"agentic-ai-s065","q":"Why validate an agent's output before it reaches downstream systems?","a":"<p>Models can produce malformed or wrong output. Schema and business-rule checks stop bad data before it triggers actions. When a check fails, retry, repair or escalate instead of passing it on.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"agentic-ai-f0250","card":"q-agentic-ai-065-robust-training-data","sec":"agentic-ai-s066","q":"Why does diverse training data make an agent more robust?","a":"<p>The agent sees a wider range of situations, so unusual inputs are less likely to be out of distribution. It generalizes better and fails less often in the field. Missing cases lead to blind spots and bias.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"agentic-ai-f0251","card":"q-agentic-ai-065-robust-training-data","sec":"agentic-ai-s066","q":"How do you check whether training data is broad enough?","a":"<p>Analyze coverage across classes, groups, edge cases and conditions. Compare the data with real production inputs, test on held-out and adversarial sets, and review failures to find missing scenarios. Use synthetic data or augmentation for rare cases.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"agentic-ai-f0252","card":"q-agentic-ai-065-robust-training-data","sec":"agentic-ai-s066","q":"Does more training data always improve robustness?","a":"<p>No. More of the same data adds little, and noisy or biased data can hurt. Coverage and quality matter more than volume. Also keep monitoring after release because the real world changes.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"agentic-ai-f0253","card":"q-agentic-ai-065-robust-training-data","sec":"agentic-ai-s066","q":"What is data poisoning, and how does it threaten training data robustness?","a":"<p>An attacker inserts bad or malicious examples into training data so the model learns wrong or hidden behaviors. Defend with provenance tracking, validation, outlier detection, access control on data pipelines and testing for backdoors.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"agentic-ai-f0254","card":"q-agentic-ai-066-system-monitoring-and-diagnostics","sec":"agentic-ai-s067","q":"What should you monitor for a production AI agent?","a":"<p>Operational metrics (latency, error rate, throughput, cost), quality metrics (task success, accuracy, user feedback), safety signals (policy violations, unusual tool use), and data and model drift. Add alerts on thresholds and trace requests across agents.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"agentic-ai-f0255","card":"q-agentic-ai-066-system-monitoring-and-diagnostics","sec":"agentic-ai-s067","q":"What is the difference between monitoring and diagnostics?","a":"<p>Monitoring continuously tracks health and performance and alerts when something is off. Diagnostics investigate why, by scanning behavior, tracing requests and performing root cause analysis. Monitoring tells you that there is a problem; diagnostics find the cause.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"agentic-ai-f0256","card":"q-agentic-ai-066-system-monitoring-and-diagnostics","sec":"agentic-ai-s067","q":"What is root cause analysis, and why does it matter for agent systems?","a":"<p>It traces a failure or wrong action back to its underlying cause, such as bad data, a prompt change, a tool outage or model drift, not just the symptom. Without it the same issue returns. Agents need good logs and traces to make it possible.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"agentic-ai-f0257","card":"q-agentic-ai-066-system-monitoring-and-diagnostics","sec":"agentic-ai-s067","q":"What is model drift, and how do you detect it in production?","a":"<p>Drift is a decline in model performance because the data or the world changed. Detect it by comparing input and output distributions with the training baseline, tracking quality metrics over time, and sampling outputs for review. Retrain or update when thresholds are crossed.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"agentic-ai-f0258","card":"q-agentic-ai-066-system-monitoring-and-diagnostics","sec":"agentic-ai-s067","q":"An agent starts giving wrong answers in production with no errors in the logs. What do you check?","a":"<p>Recent changes (model, prompt, data, tools), input distribution changes, upstream tool or API behavior, quality metrics against a baseline, and sample traces of failing cases. Compare against a known good version and roll back if needed.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"agentic-ai-f0259","card":"q-agentic-ai-067-graceful-degradation","sec":"agentic-ai-s068","q":"What is graceful degradation, and why is it better than failing completely?","a":"<p>The system keeps operating with reduced functionality when something fails, instead of shutting down. Users still get the essential service, and harm is limited while the fault is fixed. For example, falling back to simple rules when the ML model is down.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"agentic-ai-f0260","card":"q-agentic-ai-067-graceful-degradation","sec":"agentic-ai-s068","q":"What are the levels of degradation you would plan for in an agent?","a":"<p>Normal operation with full functionality, degraded mode where non-critical features are reduced or off, and a safe minimum mode that keeps only essential functions with minimum harm. Define triggers and recovery steps for moving between them.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"agentic-ai-f0261","card":"q-agentic-ai-067-graceful-degradation","sec":"agentic-ai-s068","q":"Give examples of graceful degradation techniques for an LLM-based agent.","a":"<p>Fall back to a smaller or cached model, use rule-based responses, disable optional tools, return cached or partial results, queue requests for later, and hand off to a human. Tell the user the service is in reduced mode.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"agentic-ai-f0262","card":"q-agentic-ai-067-graceful-degradation","sec":"agentic-ai-s068","q":"What is a circuit breaker, and how does it support graceful degradation?","a":"<p>A circuit breaker stops calls to a failing dependency after errors cross a threshold, then tests it again after a pause. It prevents cascading failures and wasted retries, and lets the system use a fallback while the dependency recovers.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"agentic-ai-f0263","card":"q-agentic-ai-067-graceful-degradation","sec":"agentic-ai-s068","q":"What should an autonomous agent do in safe mode when it cannot trust its own decisions?","a":"<p>Limit itself to low-risk actions, stop irreversible or high-impact actions, ask a human for approval, and keep logging. Minimum harm matters more than full functionality.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"agentic-ai-f0264","card":"q-agentic-ai-068-security-versus-reliability","sec":"agentic-ai-s069","q":"What is the difference between security and reliability in an AI system?","a":"<p>Security protects the system from threats and misuse (attackers, bad inputs, untrusted entities). Reliability keeps it running correctly despite faults and changes. A secure system can be unreliable, and a reliable one can still be insecure.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"agentic-ai-f0265","card":"q-agentic-ai-068-security-versus-reliability","sec":"agentic-ai-s069","q":"Can security and reliability goals conflict? Give an example.","a":"<p>Yes. Aggressive retries and automatic failover improve reliability but can increase attack surface, or replay unsafe requests. Strict input rejection improves security but can block valid users. Design trade-offs deliberately and test both.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"agentic-ai-f0266","card":"q-agentic-ai-068-security-versus-reliability","sec":"agentic-ai-s069","q":"Why does a denial-of-service attack count as both a security and a reliability problem?","a":"<p>It is a security attack in intent, but its effect is loss of availability, which is a reliability failure. Defenses combine rate limiting and filtering (security) with redundancy, autoscaling and graceful degradation (reliability).</p>","hand":true},{"id":"agentic-ai-f0267","card":"q-agentic-ai-069-future-capabilities-in-industry-4-0","sec":"agentic-ai-s070","q":"How does agentic AI change manufacturing in Industry 4.0?","a":"<p>Agents can analyze large data streams, decide and act in real time, and improve themselves from the results. This supports predictive maintenance, optimized production and supply chains, and human-robot collaboration, with less manual tuning.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"agentic-ai-f0268","card":"q-agentic-ai-069-future-capabilities-in-industry-4-0","sec":"agentic-ai-s070","q":"Why is real-time decision-making valuable for industrial processes?","a":"<p>Conditions such as machine state, demand or quality change quickly. Immediate decisions cut waste, prevent defects and avoid downtime. Slow decisions mean the data is stale by the time you act. It needs low-latency data and safe limits on automated actions.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"agentic-ai-f0269","card":"q-agentic-ai-069-future-capabilities-in-industry-4-0","sec":"agentic-ai-s070","q":"What are the risks of letting agents optimize industrial processes autonomously?","a":"<p>A wrong decision can damage equipment, hurt workers or halt production. Mitigate with safety limits, simulation before rollout, human override, monitoring and staged deployment. Safety-critical controls should stay deterministic and certified.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"agentic-ai-f0270","card":"q-agentic-ai-070-predictive-maintenance-and-asset-managem","sec":"agentic-ai-s071","q":"What is predictive maintenance, and how does it differ from reactive and preventive maintenance?","a":"<p>Reactive maintenance fixes things after they break. Preventive maintenance follows a fixed schedule regardless of condition. Predictive maintenance uses sensor data and models to estimate when a part will fail and schedules work just before that, cutting both unplanned downtime and unneeded servicing.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"agentic-ai-f0271","card":"q-agentic-ai-070-predictive-maintenance-and-asset-managem","sec":"agentic-ai-s071","q":"What data and techniques does predictive maintenance use?","a":"<p>Sensor data such as vibration, temperature, pressure and current, plus operating history and failure records. Techniques include anomaly detection, time-series forecasting and remaining-useful-life models. Labeled failures are rare, so unsupervised or semi-supervised methods are common.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"agentic-ai-f0272","card":"q-agentic-ai-070-predictive-maintenance-and-asset-managem","sec":"agentic-ai-s071","q":"What are the challenges of predictive maintenance?","a":"<p>Few labeled failures, noisy sensors, differing machines and conditions, false alarms that erode trust, and integration with scheduling and parts inventory. Value must be measured against the cost of sensors and models.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"agentic-ai-f0273","card":"q-agentic-ai-070-predictive-maintenance-and-asset-managem","sec":"agentic-ai-s071","q":"How does an agent add value on top of a predictive maintenance model?","a":"<p>The model predicts failure risk. The agent acts on it: schedules the work around production plans, orders parts, assigns technicians and re-plans as conditions change. It turns predictions into actions.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"agentic-ai-f0274","card":"q-agentic-ai-071-supply-chain-optimization","sec":"agentic-ai-s072","q":"How can agentic AI improve a supply chain?","a":"<p>Agents forecast demand, automate inventory and reordering, route logistics, and coordinate with many suppliers in real time. When a part is short, an agent can find alternative suppliers automatically. This cuts costs and delays and raises on-time delivery.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"agentic-ai-f0275","card":"q-agentic-ai-071-supply-chain-optimization","sec":"agentic-ai-s072","q":"Why are multi-agent systems a good fit for supply chain coordination?","a":"<p>A supply chain has many independent parties (suppliers, carriers, warehouses) with separate data and goals. Agents can represent each party, negotiate and react locally to changes, and no single central system must know everything.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"agentic-ai-f0276","card":"q-agentic-ai-071-supply-chain-optimization","sec":"agentic-ai-s072","q":"What risks come with automating procurement with agents?","a":"<p>Wrong orders at scale, fraud or manipulation by external parties, supplier data errors, and weak oversight. Use spending limits, approval for large or unusual orders, verified supplier identities and audit logs.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"agentic-ai-f0277","card":"q-agentic-ai-071-supply-chain-optimization","sec":"agentic-ai-s072","q":"What data does supply chain optimization depend on, and what happens if it is poor?","a":"<p>Demand history, inventory levels, lead times, supplier status and transport data. Poor or delayed data leads to wrong forecasts, stock-outs or excess stock. Data quality and integration are often the real bottleneck.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"agentic-ai-f0278","card":"q-agentic-ai-072-human-robot-collaboration","sec":"agentic-ai-s073","q":"How should work be divided between robots and humans in collaborative manufacturing?","a":"<p>Robots take repetitive, precise and dangerous tasks. Humans take problem-solving, creativity, handling of unusual cases and strategic decisions. Each does what it does best, which improves safety and flexibility.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"agentic-ai-f0279","card":"q-agentic-ai-072-human-robot-collaboration","sec":"agentic-ai-s073","q":"What safety measures are needed when robots work side by side with humans?","a":"<p>Force and speed limits, proximity sensors and safe stops, defined work zones, emergency stop, risk assessment and compliance with standards such as ISO 10218 and ISO/TS 15066 for collaborative robots. Safety logic should be separate from the learning components.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"agentic-ai-f0280","card":"q-agentic-ai-073-five-technology-integrations","sec":"agentic-ai-s074","q":"Which technologies are commonly combined with agentic AI, and what does each add?","a":"<p>IoT supplies data and actuators, edge computing runs decisions near the data, blockchain adds tamper-evident records, cloud gives scalable compute and updates, and 5G gives fast low-latency connectivity. Together they make agents that sense, decide and act in real time.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"agentic-ai-f0281","card":"q-agentic-ai-073-five-technology-integrations","sec":"agentic-ai-s074","q":"How would you decide which of these integrations a new agent needs?","a":"<p>Start from the requirement: need for local sensors (IoT), latency or offline use (edge), shared tamper-proof records across parties (blockchain), scale and managed services (cloud), mobility or high-bandwidth links (5G). Add only what the requirement justifies, since each adds cost and complexity.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"agentic-ai-f0282","card":"q-agentic-ai-073-five-technology-integrations","sec":"agentic-ai-s074","q":"What new risks does integration with IoT, cloud and 5G bring to an agent system?","a":"<p>A larger attack surface (many devices, networks and APIs), more dependencies that can fail, data privacy issues across providers, and difficult update management on devices. Use device identity, encryption, network segmentation and monitored updates.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"agentic-ai-f0283","card":"q-agentic-ai-074-iot-and-edge-computing","sec":"agentic-ai-s075","q":"What are the trade-offs of edge versus cloud for an agent's decisions?","a":"<p>Edge gives speed, offline operation and local data control but limited compute and model size. Cloud gives large models, easy updates and central view but adds latency and depends on connectivity. A hybrid is common: fast local decisions at the edge, heavy learning in the cloud.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"agentic-ai-f0284","card":"q-agentic-ai-074-iot-and-edge-computing","sec":"agentic-ai-s075","q":"How does IoT fit with agentic AI?","a":"<p>IoT devices (sensors, cameras, thermostats) collect data and carry out actions. The agent analyzes the data, decides and sends commands back, forming a closed loop. Without the agent, IoT only collects and reports.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"agentic-ai-f0285","card":"q-agentic-ai-074-iot-and-edge-computing","sec":"agentic-ai-s075","q":"How do you keep a fleet of edge agents updated and secure?","a":"<p>Signed over-the-air updates, staged rollouts with rollback, device identity and certificates, encrypted communication, and remote monitoring. Devices that cannot be updated become long-term security risks.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"agentic-ai-f0286","card":"q-agentic-ai-074-iot-and-edge-computing","sec":"agentic-ai-s075","q":"Which workloads belong at the edge and which in the cloud?","a":"<p>Edge: real-time control, safety reactions, pre-filtering raw sensor data. Cloud: model training, long-term analytics, coordination across sites, large model inference. Decide by latency, bandwidth, privacy and compute needs.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"agentic-ai-f0287","card":"q-agentic-ai-075-blockchain-cloud-and-5g","sec":"agentic-ai-s076","q":"What does blockchain add to an agentic AI supply chain?","a":"<p>A distributed, tamper-evident ledger that multiple parties share, so data authenticity and traceability are verifiable without a central authority. Agents can use it for settlement and audit. The costs are lower throughput, higher latency and complexity, so use it only when parties do not trust a single owner.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"agentic-ai-f0288","card":"q-agentic-ai-075-blockchain-cloud-and-5g","sec":"agentic-ai-s076","q":"When is blockchain not the right choice for agent data storage?","a":"<p>When a single trusted party controls the data, when high throughput or low latency is needed, or when data must be deleted (for example under GDPR erasure). A normal database with access control and audit logs is simpler and faster.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"agentic-ai-f0289","card":"q-agentic-ai-075-blockchain-cloud-and-5g","sec":"agentic-ai-s076","q":"Why is cloud computing useful for deploying and improving agents?","a":"<p>Elastic compute for training and inference, managed services, easy rollout of updated models and continuous learning across many agents. The trade-offs are cost, latency, vendor lock-in and data residency concerns.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"agentic-ai-f0290","card":"q-agentic-ai-075-blockchain-cloud-and-5g","sec":"agentic-ai-s076","q":"Why does 5G matter for autonomous agents such as vehicles and smart cities?","a":"<p>5G gives high bandwidth and low latency, so agents can exchange sensor data and commands in real time, with many connected devices. Safety-critical functions still need local fallbacks because connectivity can be lost.</p>","hand":true}];
